window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 2, "domain": "number", "question": "Which is smaller, -1/3 or -1/4?", "options": {"A": "-1/4.", "B": "Equal.", "C": "-1/3.", "D": "Not comparable."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-1/3): using a common denominator of 12, -1/3 becomes -4/12 and -1/4 becomes -3/12, and -4/12 sits farther left on the number line. With negative fractions, the one with the bigger size ignoring the sign is actually the smaller value. Option A has the comparison backwards, option B is wrong since the fractions have different values, and option D is incorrect because fractions can be compared directly with a common denominator."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "number", "question": "The number line above shows the values -2, -1.5, 0.4, and 2/3 plotted as points. Which list correctly orders these four numbers from smallest to largest?", "options": {"A": "-1.5, -2, 0.4, 2/3.", "B": "-2, -1.5, 2/3, 0.4.", "C": "-2, -1.5, 0.4, 2/3.", "D": "2/3, 0.4, -1.5, -2."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-2, -1.5, 0.4, 2/3): converting everything to decimals gives -2, -1.5, 0.4, and about 0.67, which line up in that exact order from smallest to largest. Option A has the two negative numbers swapped, option B puts the two positive values in the wrong order, and option D lists the numbers from largest to smallest instead of smallest to largest.", "image": "question_images/q_3.png"}, {"id": 8, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 2 - 8?", "options": {"A": "-6.", "B": "6.", "C": "-10.", "D": "10."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-6): rewriting as adding the opposite gives 2 + (-8), and since the sizes are different, subtract 2 from 8 to get 6, keeping the negative sign since 8 was the bigger size. Option B has the right size but the wrong sign, and options D and C come from adding the two sizes together instead of subtracting."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-4) × (-5)?", "options": {"A": "-20.", "B": "20.", "C": "9.", "D": "-9."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20): when multiplying two numbers with the same sign, the answer is positive, so a negative times a negative gives a positive result. Option A has the correct size but the wrong sign, and options C and D come from adding the numbers instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 10, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-4) × 5?", "options": {"A": "20.", "B": "-1.", "C": "1.", "D": "-20."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (-20): when multiplying two numbers with different signs, the answer is negative, so a negative times a positive gives a negative result. Option A has the correct size but the wrong sign, and options C and B come from subtracting the numbers instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 14, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-2) × (-3) × 5?", "options": {"A": "30.", "B": "-30.", "C": "-13.", "D": "13."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (30): count the negative signs, and since there are two (an even number), the final answer is positive. Multiplying the sizes gives 2 × 3 × 5 = 30. Option B drops the correct positive sign, and options C and D come from adding the numbers instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 1/2 + 2/3?", "options": {"A": "3/5, miscalculated.", "B": "5/6, incorrect.", "C": "1 1/6.", "D": "1 1/3."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (1 1/6): using a common denominator of 6, 1/2 becomes 3/6 and 2/3 becomes 4/6, and adding the numerators gives 7/6, which is the same as 1 and 1/6. Option A comes from adding the numerators and denominators straight across without finding a common denominator, and options B and D are close but do not match the exact sum."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 4.75 + (-1.3)?", "options": {"A": "3.45.", "B": "5.45.", "C": "6.05.", "D": "3.65."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3.45): adding a negative number is the same as subtracting, so 4.75 + (-1.3) becomes 4.75 - 1.3, which equals 3.45. Option C comes from treating the negative sign as positive and adding the two numbers together, option B has a sign error mixed into the subtraction, and option D comes from lining up the decimal points incorrectly."}, {"id": 25, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 2³ × 2⁴?", "options": {"A": "128.", "B": "4096.", "C": "2.", "D": "64."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (128): the product rule says that when multiplying powers with the same base, keep the base and add the exponents, so 2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷, which equals 128. Option B comes from multiplying the exponents instead of adding them, option C comes from subtracting the exponents, and option D is only 2⁶, one power too small."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "number", "question": "What is -3²?", "options": {"A": "9.", "B": "-6.", "C": "-9.", "D": "6."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-9): without brackets around the -3, the exponent only applies to the 3, so -3² really means -(3²) = -9. Option A confuses this with (-3)², which does equal 9 because the brackets would include the negative sign, and options B and D come from multiplying 3 × 2 instead of squaring."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "number", "question": "A submarine is at a depth of 150 metres below sea level, written as -150 metres. It then rises 40 metres. What is its new position relative to sea level?", "options": {"A": "-110.", "B": "-190.", "C": "110.", "D": "-10."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-110 metres): starting at -150 metres and rising 40 metres gives -150 + 40 = -110 metres, meaning the submarine is still 110 metres below sea level. Option B comes from subtracting 40 instead of adding it, option C drops the negative sign entirely, and option D comes from an arithmetic error in combining the two values."}, {"id": 39, "domain": "number", "question": "A recipe calls for 3/4 of a cup of flour, and a baker wants to make 1.5 times the recipe. How many cups of flour are needed?", "options": {"A": "1 1/8.", "B": "2 1/4.", "C": "1 1/4.", "D": "3/8."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 1/8 cups): since 1.5 equals 3/2, the calculation is 3/4 × 3/2 = 9/8, which equals 1 and 1/8 cups. Option C comes from a fraction multiplication error, option B comes from tripling the recipe instead of using 1.5 times, and option D comes from dividing instead of multiplying."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "number", "question": "The number line above shows two points at -3/4 and -1/2. Which statement correctly compares them?", "options": {"A": "-3/4 is greater than -1/2.", "B": "-3/4 is equal to -1/2.", "C": "-1/2 is greater than -3/4.", "D": "-1/2 is farther from zero than -3/4."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-1/2 is greater than -3/4): -1/2 equals -0.5 and -3/4 equals -0.75, and since -0.5 sits farther right on the number line, -1/2 is the larger value. Option A has the comparison backwards, option B ignores that the two points sit at different positions, and option D is false since -1/2 is actually closer to zero than -3/4.", "image": "question_images/q_44.png"}, {"id": 45, "domain": "number", "question": "A hot air balloon starts at an elevation of 250 metres. It descends 15 metres every minute for 4 minutes, then rises 2³ metres. What is its final elevation?", "options": {"A": "182.", "B": "198.", "C": "250.", "D": "130."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (198 metres): the total descent is 4 × 15 = 60 metres, and 2³ = 8 metres, so the calculation is 250 - 60 + 8 = 198 metres. Option A comes from subtracting the 2³ = 8 metre rise instead of adding it, option C forgets both the descent and the rise, and option D comes from descending for 8 minutes instead of 4, giving 250 - 15 × 8 = 130."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "number", "question": "Which list correctly orders the numbers -3.2, -3, 1/2, and -7/2 from least to greatest?", "options": {"A": "-3, -3.2, -7/2, 1/2.", "B": "-3.2, -7/2, -3, 1/2.", "C": "1/2, -3, -3.2, -7/2.", "D": "-7/2, -3.2, -3, 1/2."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (-7/2, -3.2, -3, 1/2): converting -7/2 to a decimal gives -3.5, which is the smallest, followed by -3.2, then -3, then 1/2 as the largest. Option A puts -3 before the more negative numbers, which is backwards. Option C lists the numbers from greatest to least instead of least to greatest. Option B swaps the order of -3.2 and -3.5, ignoring which one sits farther left on the number line."}, {"id": 48, "domain": "number", "question": "Point P is at -4 and point Q is at 3 on the number line above. Which point is closer to zero?", "options": {"A": "P.", "B": "Neither.", "C": "Q.", "D": "Undetermined."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Q): point Q is at 3, which has an absolute value of 3, while point P is at -4, which has an absolute value of 4. Since 3 is smaller than 4, point Q is closer to zero. Option A is wrong because P is actually farther from zero. Option B is wrong because the two points are not the same distance from zero, and option D is wrong because the distance can be found directly from the number line.", "image": "question_images/q_48.png"}, {"id": 51, "domain": "number", "question": "What is -14 - 9?", "options": {"A": "-5.", "B": "23.", "C": "5.", "D": "-23."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (-23): subtracting 9 from -14 means moving 9 more units to the left on the number line, so -14 - 9 = -23. Options A and C come from treating this like -14 + 9 instead of -14 - 9. Option B drops the negative sign entirely."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "number", "question": "A submarine is cruising at a depth of 85 metres below sea level. It rises 30 metres to avoid a reef, then descends 52 metres to continue its route. Written as a signed number, what is the submarine's new depth relative to sea level?", "options": {"A": "-167 metres.", "B": "-107 metres.", "C": "-3 metres.", "D": "107 metres."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (-107 metres): start at -85, add 30 for the rise to get -55, then subtract 52 for the descent to get -107. Option A comes from subtracting both changes instead of adding the rise first. Option C comes from adding both changes as if the submarine rose twice, calculating -85 + 30 + 52 instead of subtracting the descent. Option D drops the negative sign, showing the submarine above sea level instead of below."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-7) × (-6)?", "options": {"A": "-42.", "B": "-13.", "C": "13.", "D": "42."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (42): both numbers are negative, which is the same sign, so the product is positive, and 7 × 6 = 42. Option A keeps the correct size but gives it the wrong sign. Options C and B come from adding 7 and 6 instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-9) × 4?", "options": {"A": "36.", "B": "-13.", "C": "-36.", "D": "13."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-36): the numbers have different signs, so the product is negative, and 9 × 4 = 36. Option A keeps the correct size but makes it positive. Options B and D come from adding 9 and 4 instead of multiplying."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-72) divided by (-8)?", "options": {"A": "-9.", "B": "9.", "C": "-64.", "D": "64."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (9): both numbers are negative, which is the same sign, so the quotient is positive, and 72 divided by 8 is 9. Option A keeps the correct size but gives it the wrong sign. Options D and C come from multiplying 8 by itself instead of dividing 72 by 8."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "number", "question": "What is (-3) × (-2) × (-1) × (-5)?", "options": {"A": "-30.", "B": "30.", "C": "11.", "D": "-11."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (30): there are four negative numbers being multiplied, which is an even count, so the final answer is positive, and 3 × 2 × 1 × 5 = 30. Option A wrongly treats the count of negatives as odd. Options C and D come from adding the numbers' sizes instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 1/3 + 1/6?", "options": {"A": "2/9.", "B": "1/6.", "C": "5/6.", "D": "1/2."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (1/2): using a common denominator of 6, 1/3 becomes 2/6, and 2/6 + 1/6 = 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2. Option A comes from adding the numerators and denominators straight across (1+1 over 3+6). Option B only counts the second fraction, and option C comes from adding 2/3 and 1/6 by mistake instead of the given fractions."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "number", "question": "What is -2/3 - 1/4?", "options": {"A": "-11/12.", "B": "-5/12.", "C": "11/12.", "D": "-1/4."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-11/12): using a common denominator of 12, -2/3 becomes -8/12 and 1/4 becomes 3/12, and rewriting the subtraction as adding the opposite gives -8/12 + (-3/12) = -11/12. Option B comes from subtracting the numerators incorrectly. Option C keeps the correct size but drops the negative sign, and option D comes from subtracting only the second fraction's original denominator without converting."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 0.7 × 0.08?", "options": {"A": "0.0056.", "B": "0.56.", "C": "5.6.", "D": "0.056."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (0.056): multiply as if there were no decimal points, 7 × 8 = 56, then count the total decimal places in both numbers, which is three (one from 0.7 and two from 0.08), and place the point three spots from the right. Option B places the decimal point only two spots from the right. Options C and A place the decimal point in the wrong direction entirely."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "number", "question": "A bank account has a balance of -32.40 dollars. The owner makes a deposit of 50 dollars, then spends 12.65 dollars on groceries. What is the new balance in the account after both transactions?", "options": {"A": "-4.95 dollars.", "B": "5.05 dollars.", "C": "30.25 dollars.", "D": "4.95 dollars."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4.95 dollars): start at -32.40, add the 50 dollar deposit to get 17.60, then subtract the 12.65 dollar purchase to get 4.95. Option A gives the correct size but the wrong sign. Option C comes from adding the deposit and the purchase together instead of subtracting the purchase, and option B comes from a small subtraction error."}, {"id": 74, "domain": "number", "question": "Using exponent rules, what is 9⁸ divided by 9³ written as a single power?", "options": {"A": "9⁵.", "B": "9¹¹.", "C": "9²⁴.", "D": "81⁵."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (9⁵): when dividing powers with the same base, keep the base and subtract the exponents, 8 - 3 = 5. Option B comes from adding the exponents instead of subtracting them. Option C comes from multiplying the exponents. Option D mistakenly squares the base as well as changing the exponent."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "number", "question": "Using exponent rules, what is (5³)² written as a single power?", "options": {"A": "5⁵.", "B": "5⁶.", "C": "5⁹.", "D": "25⁶."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5⁶): when raising a power to another power, keep the base and multiply the exponents, 3 × 2 = 6. Option A comes from adding the exponents instead of multiplying them. Option C comes from raising the exponent 3² instead of multiplying it by 2. Option D mistakenly squares the base as well."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "number", "question": "Which statement correctly compares -5² and (-5)²?", "options": {"A": "Both expressions equal 25, since negatives are ignored.", "B": "-5² equals 25 and (-5)² equals -25.", "C": "-5² equals -25 and (-5)² equals 25.", "D": "Both expressions equal -25, applying the negative twice."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-5² equals -25 and (-5)² equals 25): without brackets, the exponent applies only to the 5, so -5² means -(5²) = -25, but with brackets, (-5)² means (-5) × (-5) = 25. Option A wrongly assumes the negative sign is always included in the base. Option B swaps the two results. Option D wrongly assumes the negative sign always stays outside the exponent's effect."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "number", "question": "Using compatible numbers, what is the best estimate for 123 divided by 6?", "options": {"A": "20.", "B": "15.", "C": "25.", "D": "12."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (20): using the compatible number 120 instead of 123, since 120 divides evenly by 6, gives 120 / 6 = 20, which is very close to the exact answer of 20.5. Options B, C, and D come from choosing compatible numbers that are farther away from 123, making the estimate less accurate."}, {"id": 93, "domain": "number", "question": "Simplify the ratio 24:36.", "options": {"A": "3:4.", "B": "2:3.", "C": "4:6.", "D": "3:2."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (2:3): the greatest common factor of 24 and 36 is 12, and dividing both numbers by 12 gives 2:3. Option A comes from an incorrect division of the two numbers. Option C is an equivalent ratio but has only been partly reduced, not fully simplified. Option D reverses the correct order of the simplified ratio."}, {"id": 96, "domain": "number", "question": "Which statement best describes a unit rate?", "options": {"A": "A ratio comparing two quantities with the same units.", "B": "A rate where the second quantity is 1.", "C": "A ratio comparing a part to a whole.", "D": "A percentage written as a decimal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A rate where the second quantity is 1): a unit rate scales a rate down so the second quantity equals one, making comparisons easy, like dollars per one item. Option A describes an ordinary ratio, not a rate, since rates compare quantities with different units. Option C describes a part-to-whole ratio, which is unrelated to rates. Option D describes a decimal conversion, not a unit rate."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "number", "question": "Solve for x in the proportion 5/8 = x/40.", "options": {"A": "20.", "B": "25.", "C": "32.", "D": "30."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (25): cross-multiplying gives 5 times 40 equals 8 times x, so 200 equals 8x, and dividing both sides by 8 gives x equals 25. Option A comes from dividing 40 by 2 instead of solving the proportion. Option C confuses the numerator and denominator during cross-multiplication. Option D is close but comes from a small division error."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "number", "question": "A map uses a scale of 1:200. Two landmarks are 8 cm apart on the map. What is the real distance between them?", "options": {"A": "8 m.", "B": "80 m.", "C": "160 m.", "D": "16 m."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (16 m): multiplying the map distance by the scale factor gives 8 times 200, which equals 1600 cm, and 1600 cm converts to 16 m. Option A forgets to multiply by the scale factor at all. Option B and C come from errors when converting centimeters to meters. Always apply the scale factor first, then convert units carefully."}, {"id": 111, "domain": "number", "question": "After a 25% increase, a phone costs $250. What was the original price?", "options": {"A": "$200.", "B": "$187.50.", "C": "$225.", "D": "$212.50."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ($200): setting up the equation 250 equals the original price times 1.25, and dividing both sides by 1.25 gives an original price of 200. Option B comes from mistakenly taking 25% of 250 and subtracting it from 250. Option C and D both come from applying the percent to the new price instead of solving for the original amount. The 25% increase was calculated based on the old price, not the new one, so it cannot simply be subtracted from 250."}, {"id": 112, "domain": "number", "question": "After a 40% discount, a jacket costs $54. What was the original price?", "options": {"A": "$75.60.", "B": "$135.", "C": "$81.", "D": "$90."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($90): setting up the equation 54 equals the original price times 0.60 (100% minus 40%), and dividing both sides by 0.60 gives an original price of 90. Option A comes from mistakenly adding 40% of 54 back onto 54 instead of solving the equation. Option C and B both come from using the wrong multiplier for the 40% discount. The discount percent must always be applied to the unknown original price, not to the new discounted price."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "number", "question": "An item costs $150 and is on sale for 25% off. After the discount, 13% HST is added to the sale price. What is the final price?", "options": {"A": "$169.50.", "B": "$132.00.", "C": "$112.50.", "D": "$127.13."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($127.13): applying the discount first gives 150 times 0.75, which equals 112.50, and then applying the tax gives 112.50 times 1.13, which equals 127.13. Option B comes from wrongly combining the discount and tax into a single net 12% change instead of applying each step separately. Option C only applies the discount and forgets to add the HST afterward. Option A only applies the tax to the original price and forgets to apply the discount at all."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "number", "question": "A recipe for 5 people uses 3 cups of rice. How much rice is needed to serve 12 people using the same recipe?", "options": {"A": "6 cups.", "B": "6.5 cups.", "C": "7 cups.", "D": "7.2 cups."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (7.2 cups): the scale factor is 12 divided by 5, which equals 2.4, and multiplying 3 cups by 2.4 gives 7.2 cups. Option A comes from doubling the recipe instead of using the correct scale factor. Option B and C both come from rounding the scale factor before multiplying. Always calculate the exact scale factor first, then multiply every ingredient by that same value."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "number", "question": "A fruit bowl has 6 apples and 9 oranges. What is the ratio, in simplest form, of apples to the total number of pieces of fruit?", "options": {"A": "2:5.", "B": "6:9.", "C": "3:5.", "D": "6:15."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (2:5): the total number of fruit is 6 plus 9, which equals 15, so the part-to-whole ratio is 6:15, and simplifying by dividing both numbers by 3 gives 2:5. Option B is the part-to-part ratio of apples to oranges, not apples to the whole. Option C comes from an incorrect simplification of 6:15. Option D is the correct part-to-whole ratio but has not been simplified."}, {"id": 121, "domain": "number", "question": "A recipe ratio is written as flour to sugar is 3:2. If someone accidentally writes it as 2:3 instead, what have they done?", "options": {"A": "Nothing, the ratio still means exactly the same thing.", "B": "Simplified the ratio correctly, since the order never matters.", "C": "Reversed the order, making it compare sugar to flour instead.", "D": "Created an equivalent ratio by simply swapping the two numbers."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reversed the order, making it compare sugar to flour instead): switching the two numbers changes what is being compared to what, so 2:3 now describes sugar to flour rather than flour to sugar. Option A is wrong because ratio order always matters and changes the meaning. Option B is incorrect because no simplification took place, since 3:2 was already in simplest form. Option D is incorrect because an equivalent ratio must represent the same comparison, and reversing the order does not."}, {"id": 123, "domain": "number", "question": "A classroom has students in a ratio of boys to girls of 3:5. If there are 32 students in total, how many girls are there?", "options": {"A": "12.", "B": "20.", "C": "15.", "D": "18."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20): the ratio parts add up to 3 plus 5, which equals 8, and dividing the total of 32 by 8 gives 4 students per part, so the girls are 5 times 4, which equals 20. Option A is the number of boys, not girls. Option C and D both come from using the wrong value for each part before multiplying. Always divide the total by the sum of the ratio parts first, then multiply by the specific part being asked about."}, {"id": 132, "domain": "number", "question": "A total receipt including 13% HST is $101.70. What was the price of the item before tax?", "options": {"A": "$88.50.", "B": "$92.30.", "C": "$90.00.", "D": "$114.92."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($90.00): since the total equals the original price times 1.13, dividing 101.70 by 1.13 gives an original price of 90.00. Option A comes from mistakenly subtracting 13% of the total instead of dividing by 1.13. Option B comes from a small division error. Option D mistakenly multiplies by 1.13 again instead of dividing."}, {"id": 138, "domain": "number", "question": "Which ratio is equivalent to 3:5?", "options": {"A": "6:10.", "B": "3:8.", "C": "5:3.", "D": "8:10."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (6:10): Multiplying both numbers in 3:5 by 2 gives 6:10, an equivalent ratio. Option C reverses the order of the original ratio. Option B incorrectly adds instead of multiplying. Option D changes only one of the two numbers, which breaks the equivalent relationship."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "number", "question": "A recipe uses flour and sugar in a ratio of 4:3. If you use 12 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar are needed to keep the same ratio?", "options": {"A": "3.", "B": "9.", "C": "12.", "D": "8."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (9): Since 12 cups of flour is 3 times the original 4 cups (12 divided by 4 equals 3), the sugar amount must also be multiplied by 3, giving 3 times 3 equals 9 cups. Option A is just the scale factor, not the final sugar amount. Option D incorrectly adds instead of multiplying. Option C mistakenly repeats the flour amount."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "number", "question": "A 6 pack of juice boxes costs $3.60. What is the unit price per juice box?", "options": {"A": "$0.36.", "B": "$6.00.", "C": "$21.60.", "D": "$0.60."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($0.60): Dividing the total cost by the number of juice boxes gives 3.60 divided by 6 equals $0.60 per box. Option A misplaces the decimal point. Option C mistakenly multiplies instead of dividing. Option B confuses the number of boxes with the price."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "number", "question": "What is 12% of 150?", "options": {"A": "18.", "B": "138.", "C": "162.", "D": "12."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (18): Converting 12% to a decimal gives 0.12, and multiplying by 150 gives 0.12 times 150 equals 18. Option D repeats the percent value itself. Option B mistakenly subtracts 12 from 150 instead of finding the percent. Option C mistakenly adds 12 to 150 instead of finding the percent."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "number", "question": "A jacket originally costs $90. It goes on sale for 40% off. What is the sale price?", "options": {"A": "$36.", "B": "$54.", "C": "$50.", "D": "$126."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($54): The decrease is 0.40 times 90 equals $36, and subtracting that from the original price gives 90 minus 36 equals $54. Option A is only the amount of the decrease, not the final price. Option C is a rounding guess that does not match the actual calculation. Option D mistakenly adds the decrease instead of subtracting it."}, {"id": 152, "domain": "number", "question": "After a 15% increase, the price of a bicycle helmet is now $115. What was the original price?", "options": {"A": "$97.75.", "B": "$100.", "C": "$132.25.", "D": "$110."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($100): Since the new price equals the original times 1.15 (100% plus 15%), dividing 115 by 1.15 gives the original price of $100. Option A incorrectly subtracts 15% of the new price from the new price. Option C incorrectly adds 15% of the new price to the new price. Option D is a guess that does not check out against the 15% increase."}, {"id": 153, "domain": "number", "question": "After a 25% discount, a pair of shoes costs $60. What was the original price?", "options": {"A": "$75.", "B": "$80.", "C": "$85.", "D": "$45."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($80): since the sale price equals the original price times 0.75 (100% minus 25%), dividing 60 by 0.75 gives the original price of $80. Option A incorrectly adds 25% of the sale price to the sale price (60 × 1.25 = 75) instead of dividing by 0.75. Option C looks close but does not check out, since 85 × 0.75 = 63.75, not 60. Option D mistakenly applies the 25% discount again to the sale price (60 × 0.75 = 45) instead of working backward to the original price."}, {"id": 158, "domain": "number", "question": "Convert 35% to a simplified fraction.", "options": {"A": "7/20.", "B": "1/35.", "C": "35/1.", "D": "35/10."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (7/20): Writing 35% as 35/100 and dividing both numbers by their greatest common factor of 5 gives 7/20. Option C forgets to divide by 100 at all. Option B flips the fraction upside down. Option D divides by an incorrect common factor."}, {"id": 165, "domain": "number", "question": "A paint mix uses red and yellow paint in a ratio of 7:5. You have 21 litres of red paint. How much yellow paint do you need to keep the same ratio?", "options": {"A": "21 L.", "B": "9 L.", "C": "15 L.", "D": "35 L."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (15 L): The scale factor is found by dividing the new red amount by the original, 21 divided by 7 equals 3, and multiplying the yellow amount by that same factor gives 5 times 3 equals 15 L. Option B comes from subtracting instead of using the scale factor correctly. Option A repeats the amount of red paint instead of calculating yellow. Option D multiplies both ratio numbers together instead of applying the scale factor."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "number", "question": "Store A sells juice at 3 L for $4.50. Store B sells the same juice at 2 L for $3.20. Which store has the better price per litre?", "options": {"A": "Store B, at $1.60 per litre.", "B": "Store A, at $1.50 per litre.", "C": "They cost the same per litre.", "D": "Store B."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Store A, at $1.50 per litre): Store A costs 4.50 divided by 3 equals $1.50 per litre, while Store B costs 3.20 divided by 2 equals $1.60 per litre, so Store A has the lower unit price. Option A states the wrong store even though the unit price shown is correct for Store B. Option C is incorrect because the two unit prices are not equal. Option D confuses a lower total price with a lower unit price, which is not the same thing."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "number", "question": "A recipe uses butter and flour in a ratio of 1:3. If you have 12 cups of flour, how much butter do you need to keep the same ratio?", "options": {"A": "3 cups.", "B": "12 cups.", "C": "4 cups.", "D": "36 cups."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (4 cups): The scale factor is 12 divided by 3 equals 4, and multiplying the butter amount by that same factor gives 1 times 4 equals 4 cups. Option A repeats the original ratio number for flour instead of scaling butter. Option B repeats the amount of flour rather than calculating butter. Option D mistakenly multiplies the flour amount by the ratio number instead of using the scale factor correctly."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "number", "question": "What is a unit rate?", "options": {"A": "A rate that compares two different items with no numbers.", "B": "A ratio written only as a fraction.", "C": "A rate where both quantities are greater than one.", "D": "A rate where the second quantity is 1."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (a rate where the second quantity is 1): A unit rate scales a rate down so the second quantity becomes exactly one, like kilometers per one hour. Option A is not a real mathematical definition and describes nothing measurable. Option B describes a way to write a ratio, not specifically a unit rate. Option C is incorrect because a unit rate specifically requires the second quantity to equal one, not simply be greater than one."}, {"id": 190, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Which of the following is a like term to 8x²?", "options": {"A": "8x.", "B": "2x³.", "C": "8xy.", "D": "-3x²."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (-3x²): like terms must have the exact same variable part, including the same exponent, and -3x² has x² just like 8x². Option A has x¹, which is a different exponent, so it is not a like term even though the coefficient looks similar. Option C has both x and y, which is a different variable part entirely. Option B has x³, which again does not match the exponent of 2."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify: 7a + 2b - 3a + 5b", "options": {"A": "10ab.", "B": "4a + 7b.", "C": "4a - 7b.", "D": "4ab + 7ab."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (4a + 7b): grouping the a terms gives 7a - 3a = 4a, and grouping the b terms gives 2b + 5b = 7b, so the answer stays as two separate terms, 4a + 7b. Option A wrongly combines a and b into a single ab term, but a and b are different variables and cannot be merged. Option C has a sign error on the b terms. Option D repeats the same mistake as option A by treating a and b as if they were the same variable."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Add: (4x² + 3x - 2) + (2x² - 5x + 6)", "options": {"A": "6x² - 2x + 4.", "B": "6x² - 2x - 4.", "C": "6x² + 2x + 4.", "D": "2x² - 2x + 4."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (6x² - 2x + 4): combine the x² terms to get 4x² + 2x² = 6x², combine the x terms to get 3x - 5x = -2x, and combine the constants to get -2 + 6 = 4, giving 6x² - 2x + 4. Option C makes a sign error combining the x terms. Option B makes a sign error on the constant instead. Option D makes an arithmetic error on the x² terms, dropping one of them entirely."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply: 3x(2x² - 5x + 4)", "options": {"A": "6x² - 15x² + 12x.", "B": "6x³ - 15x² + 12x.", "C": "6x³ - 15x + 12x.", "D": "6x³ - 8x² + 12x."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (6x³ - 15x² + 12x): multiply 3x by each term, using the product rule to add exponents on x each time: 3x times 2x² is 6x³, 3x times -5x is -15x², and 3x times 4 is 12x, giving 6x³ - 15x² + 12x. Option A makes an exponent error on the first term, forgetting to add the exponents correctly. Option C makes an exponent error on the second term instead. Option D makes an arithmetic error multiplying the coefficients of the middle term."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply: -2x²(3x - 4)", "options": {"A": "-6x³ + 8x².", "B": "-6x³ - 8x².", "C": "6x³ + 8x².", "D": "-6x⁵ + 8x⁴."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-6x³ + 8x²): multiply -2x² by 3x to get -6x³, and multiply -2x² by -4, and a negative times a negative gives a positive, so that becomes +8x², giving -6x³ + 8x². Option B makes a sign error on the second term. Option C makes a sign error on the first term instead. Option D incorrectly multiplies the exponents together instead of adding them, which is the wrong rule for this situation."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply: (2x - 3)(x - 4)", "options": {"A": "2x² - 5x + 12.", "B": "2x² - 11x - 12.", "C": "2x² + 11x + 12.", "D": "2x² - 11x + 12."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2x² - 11x + 12): the first terms give 2x², the outer terms give -8x, the inner terms give -3x, and the last terms give +12, and combining -8x - 3x gives -11x, so the answer is 2x² - 11x + 12. Option A makes an arithmetic error combining the middle terms. Option B makes a sign error on the last term, treating negative times negative as negative. Option C makes a sign error on the middle term."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify using the exponent laws: x⁹ divided by x⁴", "options": {"A": "x¹³.", "B": "x².", "C": "x⁵.", "D": "x³⁶."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (x⁵): the quotient rule says that when dividing powers with the same base, you subtract the exponents, so 9 - 4 = 5, giving x⁵. Option A incorrectly adds the exponents instead of subtracting, which is the rule for multiplying powers, not dividing them. Option B makes an arithmetic error in the subtraction. Option D incorrectly multiplies the exponents together, which is the rule for a power raised to another power."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify: (3x²)(4x⁵)", "options": {"A": "7x⁷.", "B": "12x¹⁰.", "C": "12x⁷.", "D": "12x³."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (12x⁷): multiply the coefficients, 3 times 4 equals 12, and add the exponents, 2 + 5 equals 7, giving 12x⁷. Option A incorrectly adds the coefficients instead of multiplying them. Option B incorrectly multiplies the exponents together instead of adding them. Option D incorrectly subtracts the exponents instead of adding them."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Factor: 8x + 12", "options": {"A": "8(x + 12).", "B": "2(4x + 6).", "C": "4(2x + 12).", "D": "4(2x + 3)."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4(2x + 3)): the greatest common factor of 8 and 12 is 4, and dividing each term by 4 gives 2x and 3, so the fully factored form is 4(2x + 3). Option B uses 2 instead of the greatest common factor, so the bracket still has a common factor left inside it and is not fully factored. Option C forgets to divide the second term by the common factor at all. Option A uses a factor that is too large, since 8 does not divide evenly into 12."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Factor: 10x² - 15x", "options": {"A": "5x(2x - 3).", "B": "5(2x² - 3x).", "C": "5x(2x + 3).", "D": "x(10x - 15)."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5x(2x - 3)): the greatest common factor of the coefficients 10 and 15 is 5, and since both terms have at least one x, the overall greatest common factor is 5x, and dividing each term by 5x gives 2x and -3, so the factored form is 5x(2x - 3). Option B only pulls out the number 5 and forgets to include the common x, so it is not fully factored. Option C makes a sign error on the second term inside the brackets. Option D uses a common factor that is too small, since 5 also divides evenly into both coefficients."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Evaluate 4x - 7 when x = 6", "options": {"A": "17.", "B": "10.", "C": "3.", "D": "24."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (17): substitute 6 for x to get 4(6) - 7, and following the order of operations, multiply first to get 24, then subtract 7 to get 17. Option D only performs the multiplication and forgets to subtract 7 at the end. Option B makes an arithmetic error in the final subtraction. Option C makes a larger arithmetic error, subtracting far too much from 24."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Which expression represents \"twice a number, decreased by five, then multiplied by three\"?", "options": {"A": "6n - 5.", "B": "3(2n - 5).", "C": "2(3n - 5).", "D": "2n - 15."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (3(2n - 5)): the word \"then\" tells you the subtraction happens first inside brackets, giving (2n - 5), and the whole quantity is multiplied by three, giving 3(2n - 5). Option A forgets the brackets and only multiplies the first term, 2n, by three while leaving the 5 alone. Option C mixes up which number gets doubled and which quantity gets tripled. Option D only multiplies the 5 by three instead of multiplying the entire bracket by three."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Which set of words correctly describes the expression 5x - 2?", "options": {"A": "Two more than five times a number.", "B": "Five less than two times a number.", "C": "Two less than five times a number.", "D": "The difference of five and two times a number."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two less than five times a number): 5x means five times a number, and subtracting 2 from that result matches \"two less than\" placed after it. Option B swaps the roles of five and two, describing 2x - 5 instead of 5x - 2. Option A uses \"more than,\" which would require addition instead of the subtraction shown in the expression. Option D describes 5 minus 2x, which reverses both the order of the subtraction and which number multiplies the variable compared with 5x - 2."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Expand: 2(4x² - 3x + 1)", "options": {"A": "8x² - 6x + 1.", "B": "8x² - 3x + 1.", "C": "8x² - 6x + 2.", "D": "6x² - 6x + 2."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (8x² - 6x + 2): multiply the 2 across all three terms, giving 2 times 4x² equals 8x², 2 times -3x equals -6x, and 2 times 1 equals 2. Option B only multiplies the first term by 2 and leaves the last two terms unchanged. Option A multiplies the first two terms correctly but forgets to multiply the final 1 by 2. Option D makes an arithmetic error on the first term, computing 2 times 4 as 6 instead of 8."}, {"id": 239, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Expand: -5(2x + 3)", "options": {"A": "-10x - 15.", "B": "-10x + 15.", "C": "10x - 15.", "D": "-10x - 3."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-10x - 15): -5 times 2x gives -10x, and -5 times 3 gives -15, since a negative times a positive is negative. Option B keeps the first term correct but incorrectly flips the sign of the second term to positive. Option C flips the sign of the first term instead of keeping it negative. Option D only multiplies the first term by -5 and leaves the 3 unmultiplied."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Add: (2x² + 3x - 4) + (x² - 5x + 6)", "options": {"A": "3x² - 2x + 2.", "B": "3x² - 2x - 10.", "C": "x² - 2x + 2.", "D": "3x² + 8x + 2."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3x² - 2x + 2): drop the brackets and group like terms, (2x² + x²) gives 3x², (3x - 5x) gives -2x, and (-4 + 6) gives 2. Option D makes a sign error on the x terms, adding 3 and 5 instead of combining 3x with -5x. Option B correctly finds the x² and x terms but makes a sign error on the constant, treating -4 + 6 as -10. Option C makes an error on the x² coefficient, treating 2x² + x² as only x²."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Subtract: (4x² - 2x + 3) - (x² + 3x - 5)", "options": {"A": "3x² + x + 8.", "B": "3x² - 5x - 2.", "C": "5x² - 5x + 8.", "D": "3x² - 5x + 8."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3x² - 5x + 8): distribute the negative sign across the second bracket first, turning it into -x² - 3x + 5, then combine like terms, (4x² - x²) gives 3x², (-2x - 3x) gives -5x, and (3 + 5) gives 8. Option B correctly finds the x² and x terms but makes a sign error on the constant, computing 3 - 5 instead of 3 + 5. Option C makes an error on the x² terms, adding 4 and 1 instead of subtracting. Option A fails to distribute the negative sign to the 3x term, treating -2x - 3x as -2x + 3x."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Subtract: (3x² + x - 6) - (2x² - 4x + 1)", "options": {"A": "x² - 3x - 7.", "B": "5x² + 5x - 7.", "C": "x² + 5x - 7.", "D": "x² + 5x - 5."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (x² + 5x - 7): distribute the negative sign across the second bracket, turning it into -2x² + 4x - 1, then combine like terms, (3x² - 2x²) gives x², (x + 4x) gives 5x, and (-6 - 1) gives -7. Option A fails to flip the sign of the -4x term, treating x + (-4x) as -3x instead of x + 4x. Option B makes an error on the x² terms, adding 3 and 2 instead of subtracting. Option D makes a sign error on the constant, computing -6 + 1 instead of -6 - 1."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Add: (7a - 2b + 3) + (-3a + 5b - 4)", "options": {"A": "4a - 3b - 1.", "B": "10a + 3b - 1.", "C": "4a + 7b - 1.", "D": "4a + 3b - 1."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4a + 3b - 1): combine the a terms, (7a - 3a) gives 4a, the b terms, (-2b + 5b) gives 3b, and the constants, (3 - 4) gives -1. Option A correctly finds 4a and -1 but makes a sign error on the b term. Option B makes an error on the a terms, adding 7 and 3 instead of subtracting. Option C makes an error combining the b terms, treating -2 + 5 as 7 instead of 3."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply: 6x²(2x - 3)", "options": {"A": "8x³ - 18x².", "B": "12x² - 18x².", "C": "12x³ - 18x².", "D": "12x³ - 18x."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (12x³ - 18x²): 6x² times 2x gives 12x³ (add the exponents 2 and 1), and 6x² times -3 gives -18x². Option B makes an exponent error on the first term, forgetting to add the exponents so it stays as x². Option A makes a coefficient error on the first term, computing 6 times 2 as 8 instead of 12. Option D makes an exponent error on the second term, dropping the x² down to a plain x."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply: (2x + 1)(x - 5)", "options": {"A": "2x² - 9x + 5.", "B": "2x² + 9x - 5.", "C": "2x² - 9x - 5.", "D": "2x² - 11x - 5."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (2x² - 9x - 5): multiplying out gives 2x², -10x, x, and -5, and collecting -10x and x gives -9x, so the answer is 2x² - 9x - 5. Option A keeps the middle term correct but flips the sign of the last term to positive. Option B flips the sign of the middle term to positive instead of keeping it negative. Option D makes an error combining the middle terms, treating -10x and x as if both were negative and adding to -11x."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify: 15x⁹ divided by 3x³", "options": {"A": "5x⁶.", "B": "12x⁶.", "C": "5x³.", "D": "5x²⁷."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5x⁶): divide the coefficients, 15 divided by 3 gives 5, and subtract the exponents, 9 - 3 gives 6, so the answer is 5x⁶. Option C correctly divides the coefficients but incorrectly divides the exponents instead of subtracting them. Option B incorrectly subtracts the coefficients, 15 - 3, instead of dividing them. Option D correctly divides the coefficients but incorrectly multiplies the exponents instead of subtracting them."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Factor completely: 6x + 15", "options": {"A": "3(2x + 15).", "B": "6(x + 15).", "C": "3(2x + 5).", "D": "2(3x + 7)."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3(2x + 5)): the greatest common factor of 6 and 15 is 3, and dividing each term by 3 gives 2x and 5, so the fully factored form is 3(2x + 5). You can check by expanding, since 3 times 2x is 6x and 3 times 5 is 15. Option A only divides the first term by 3. Option D expands to 6x + 14, which does not match the original expression."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Factor completely: 6x² - 9x", "options": {"A": "3(2x² - 3x).", "B": "3x(2x - 3).", "C": "3x(2x - 9).", "D": "x(6x - 9)."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (3x(2x - 3)): the greatest common factor of 6x² and 9x is 3x, and dividing each term by 3x gives 2x and -3, so the factored form is 3x(2x - 3). Option A only pulls out the numeric factor of 3 and leaves an x still inside both terms, which is not the greatest common factor. Option C correctly factors out 3x but incorrectly divides -9x by 3x as -9 instead of -3. Option D only pulls out an x and misses the numeric factor of 3 that both coefficients share."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Evaluate 5x - 2y when x = -2 and y = 3.", "options": {"A": "-16.", "B": "-4.", "C": "16.", "D": "4."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-16): substitute the values to get 5(-2) - 2(3), which is -10 - 6, equal to -16. Option B makes a sign error, treating -10 - 6 as -10 + 6 instead. Option C flips the overall sign of the correct answer. Option D drops the negative sign on the first term entirely, treating 5(-2) as +10 instead of -10."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A student is asked to write a word phrase that matches the expression 6x - 11. Which phrase below has the same meaning as this expression?", "options": {"A": "Six less than eleven times a number.", "B": "Eleven less than six times a number.", "C": "The product of six and eleven, decreased by a number.", "D": "Eleven decreased by six times a number."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Eleven less than six times a number): six times a number is 6x, and \"eleven less than\" means subtracting 11 from that, which matches 6x - 11 exactly. Option A swaps the six and eleven, producing 11x - 6 instead. Option C describes multiplying six and eleven together, which is not what the expression shows. Option D reverses the subtraction order, which would mean 11 - 6x."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Which pair of terms below are like terms?", "options": {"A": "4x and 4x².", "B": "7xy and -2xy.", "C": "5x and 5y.", "D": "3a²b and 3ab²."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (7xy and -2xy): both terms have the exact same variable part, xy, so they are like terms even though their coefficients differ. Option A has different exponents on x, so the variable parts do not match. Option C has completely different variables, x versus y. Option D looks similar but the exponents on a and b are swapped between the two terms, so the variable parts are not identical."}, {"id": 277, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify the expression 9x + 5x - 3x by collecting like terms.", "options": {"A": "9x.", "B": "8x.", "C": "11x.", "D": "11x²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (11x): all three terms have x¹, so they are like terms, and adding the coefficients gives 9 + 5 - 3 = 11, so the answer is 11x. Option A only keeps the first term and ignores the others. Option B makes a small addition and subtraction error. Option D incorrectly raises the exponent to 2, which only happens when multiplying powers, not when adding like terms."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Expand the expression -3(x² - 4x + 2) using the distributive property.", "options": {"A": "-3x² - 12x - 6.", "B": "-3x² + 12x + 6.", "C": "-3x² + 12x - 6.", "D": "3x² + 12x - 6."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-3x² + 12x - 6): multiply -3 by each term, -3 times x² is -3x², -3 times -4x is +12x, and -3 times 2 is -6. Option A keeps the middle term negative instead of flipping it to positive. Option B flips the sign of the last term incorrectly. Option D makes a sign error on the very first term, which should remain negative."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Add the polynomials (4x² + 3x - 2) + (2x² - 5x + 6).", "options": {"A": "6x² + 8x + 4.", "B": "6x² - 2x + 4.", "C": "2x² - 2x + 4.", "D": "6x² - 2x - 4."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (6x² - 2x + 4): drop the brackets and collect like terms, (4x² + 2x²) = 6x², (3x - 5x) = -2x, and (-2 + 6) = 4, giving 6x² - 2x + 4. Option A adds the x coefficients incorrectly instead of subtracting. Option D gets the correct x² and x terms but makes a sign error on the constant. Option C forgets to add the x² terms correctly."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply the monomial by the polynomial: -3x(4x - 6).", "options": {"A": "-12x² - 18x.", "B": "12x² + 18x.", "C": "-12x² + 18x.", "D": "-7x² + 18x."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-12x² + 18x): multiply -3x by 4x to get -12x², and multiply -3x by -6 to get +18x, since a negative times a negative is positive. Option A keeps the second term negative instead of flipping its sign. Option B makes a sign error on the first term. Option D incorrectly adds -3 and 4 instead of multiplying them for the first term."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply the monomial by the polynomial: 2x²(3x² - 4x + 1).", "options": {"A": "6x² - 8x + 2.", "B": "6x⁴ - 8x² + 2x².", "C": "6x⁴ - 8x³ + 2x².", "D": "5x⁴ - 8x³ + 2x²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (6x⁴ - 8x³ + 2x²): multiply 2x² by each term, adding exponents each time, 2x² times 3x² is 6x⁴, 2x² times -4x is -8x³, and 2x² times 1 is 2x². Option A forgets to add the exponents on any of the terms. Option B adds the exponents incorrectly on the middle term. Option D multiplies the coefficients wrong on the first term, using 5 instead of 6."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply the two binomials: (x + 4)(x + 6).", "options": {"A": "x² + 24x + 10.", "B": "2x + 10.", "C": "x² + 10x + 10.", "D": "x² + 10x + 24."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (x² + 10x + 24): multiply first, outer, inner, and last terms, x times x is x², x times 6 is 6x, 4 times x is 4x, and 4 times 6 is 24, then combine 6x + 4x into 10x, giving x² + 10x + 24. Option A swaps the middle and last term values by mistake. Option C forgets to multiply the last terms correctly. Option B forgets to multiply the first terms entirely and only adds the outer numbers."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Multiply the two binomials: (3x - 2)(2x - 1).", "options": {"A": "6x² - 7x - 2.", "B": "6x² - x + 2.", "C": "6x² - 7x + 2.", "D": "5x² - 7x + 2."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (6x² - 7x + 2): first terms give 6x², outer terms give -3x, inner terms give -4x, and last terms give +2, and combining -3x - 4x gives -7x, so the answer is 6x² - 7x + 2. Option B incorrectly combines -3x and -4x as -x instead of -7x. Option A makes a sign error on the constant term. Option D multiplies the first terms incorrectly, using 5x² instead of 6x²."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A garden bed is (x + 5) metres long and (x + 2) metres wide. Which expanded expression represents its area?", "options": {"A": "x² + 7x + 10.", "B": "x² + 7x + 7.", "C": "x² + 10x + 7.", "D": "2x + 7."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (x² + 7x + 10): area equals length times width, (x + 5)(x + 2), and multiplying first, outer, inner, and last terms gives x² + 2x + 5x + 10, which combines to x² + 7x + 10. Option B makes an error on the last term, using 7 instead of 10. Option C swaps the middle and last term values. Option D forgets to multiply the first terms together entirely."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify the expression (3x⁴)(5x²) using the exponent laws.", "options": {"A": "15x⁸.", "B": "8x⁶.", "C": "15x².", "D": "15x⁶."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (15x⁶): multiply the coefficients, 3 times 5 is 15, and add the exponents, 4 + 2 is 6, giving 15x⁶. Option A correctly multiplies the coefficients but incorrectly multiplies the exponents instead of adding them. Option B incorrectly adds the coefficients instead of multiplying them. Option C multiplies the coefficients correctly but forgets to add the exponents at all."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify the expression x¹² divided by x⁵ using the exponent laws.", "options": {"A": "x¹⁷.", "B": "x⁷.", "C": "x⁵.", "D": "x⁶⁰."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (x⁷): when dividing powers with the same base, subtract the exponents, so 12 - 5 gives 7, and the answer is x⁷. Option A adds the exponents, which is the rule for multiplying powers. Option D multiplies the exponents, which is the rule for a power raised to another power."}, {"id": 304, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify the expression (x⁵)⁴ using the exponent laws.", "options": {"A": "x²⁰.", "B": "x⁶²⁵.", "C": "x⁹.", "D": "x⁵."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (x²⁰): when a power is raised to another power, multiply the exponents, so 5 times 4 gives 20, and the answer is x²⁰. Option C adds the exponents instead of multiplying them. Option B raises 5 to the fourth power, which mixes up the base and the exponent."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Simplify the expression 18x⁹ divided by 6x³ using the exponent laws.", "options": {"A": "3x³.", "B": "12x⁶.", "C": "3x⁶.", "D": "3x²⁷."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3x⁶): divide the coefficients, 18 divided by 6 is 3, and subtract the exponents, 9 - 3 is 6, giving 3x⁶. Option A divides the coefficients correctly but forgets to subtract the exponents properly. Option B incorrectly subtracts the coefficients instead of dividing them. Option D divides the coefficients correctly but multiplies the exponents instead of subtracting them."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Evaluate the expression 5a - 2b when a = -2 and b = 3.", "options": {"A": "-16.", "B": "16.", "C": "-4.", "D": "4."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (-16): substitute the values to get 5(-2) - 2(3), which is -10 - 6, giving -16. Option C makes a sign error and combines the values incorrectly. Option B flips the overall sign of the correct answer. Option D also flips the sign but is off by a smaller error in the middle step."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "algebra", "question": "When solving a linear equation, what does it mean to isolate the variable?", "options": {"A": "Getting the variable alone on one side of the equation.", "B": "Making the variable disappear completely from the equation entirely.", "C": "Multiplying both sides of the equation by the variable itself.", "D": "Changing the variable to a completely different letter or symbol."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Getting the variable alone on one side of the equation). Isolating the variable means using opposite operations to undo everything attached to it until it stands by itself. Option B is wrong because the variable does not disappear, it becomes equal to a number. Option C is wrong because multiplying by the variable would make the equation harder to solve, not easier. Option D is wrong because changing the letter does not solve anything."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve the equation with brackets: 3(x + 2) = 2x + 10", "options": {"A": "x = 8.", "B": "x = 16.", "C": "x = -16.", "D": "x = 4."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (x = 4). Expanding the left side gives 3x + 6 = 2x + 10, then subtracting 2x from both sides gives x + 6 = 10, and subtracting 6 gives x = 4. Option A comes from distributing incorrectly as 3x + 2 instead of 3x + 6. Options B and C come from mixing up the order of the subtraction steps."}, {"id": 324, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve the equation with variables on both sides: 5x - 3 = 2x + 12", "options": {"A": "x = 3.", "B": "x = 5.", "C": "x = 9.", "D": "x = 15."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (x = 5). Subtracting 2x from both sides gives 3x - 3 = 12, then adding 3 gives 3x = 15, and dividing by 3 gives x = 5. Option D is the value of 3x before dividing by 3. Options A and C come from adding or subtracting the constants in the wrong order."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve the equation with variables on both sides: 7x - 4 = 3x + 16", "options": {"A": "x = 4.", "B": "x = 3.", "C": "x = 5.", "D": "x = 20."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (x = 5). Subtracting 3x from both sides gives 4x - 4 = 16, then adding 4 gives 4x = 20, and dividing by 4 gives x = 5. Option D is the value of 4x before dividing by 4. Options A and B come from arithmetic slips when combining the constant terms."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Is x = 6 a solution to the equation 2x + 3 = 15?", "options": {"A": "No, because 2(6) + 3 = 9.", "B": "Yes, because 6 + 3 = 15.", "C": "No, because 2(6) = 15.", "D": "Yes, because 2(6) + 3 = 15."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yes, because 2(6) + 3 = 15). Substituting x = 6 gives 2(6) + 3 = 12 + 3 = 15, which matches the right side, so x = 6 is a correct solution. Option A miscalculates 2(6) + 3 as 9. Options B and C skip or misuse the multiplication step entirely."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The points (0,5), (1,8), (2,11), and (3,14) are plotted on the grid below and connected by a straight line. Which equation matches this linear relation?", "options": {"A": "y = 5x + 3.", "B": "y = 3x + 5.", "C": "y = 3x - 5.", "D": "y = 2x + 5."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (y = 3x + 5). The y-values increase by 3 each time x increases by 1, so the slope is 3, and the y-value when x = 0 is 5, so the y-intercept is 5, giving y = 3x + 5. Option A swaps the slope and y-intercept values. Option C uses the wrong sign for the y-intercept. Option D uses the wrong slope value.", "image": "question_images/q_336.png"}, {"id": 344, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What does a slope of zero represent?", "options": {"A": "A vertical line.", "B": "A horizontal, flat line.", "C": "A line that rises steeply.", "D": "A line that falls steeply."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A horizontal, flat line). A slope of zero means y never changes as x changes, so the line stays perfectly flat. Option A describes an undefined slope instead. Options C and D describe lines with large positive or negative slopes, not a slope of zero."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "algebra", "question": "In slope-intercept form, y = mx + b, what does b represent?", "options": {"A": "The y-intercept.", "B": "The x-value.", "C": "The slope.", "D": "The variable."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The y-intercept). In this form b always stands for the starting value of y when x is 0. Option C describes m, not b. Option B is incorrect since x is already its own separate variable in the formula. Option D is too vague and does not identify what b specifically represents."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph below shows a line crossing the y-axis at (0,4) and passing through (2,8). What is the equation of this line?", "options": {"A": "y = 2x + 4.", "B": "y = 4x + 2.", "C": "y = 2x + 8.", "D": "y = 4x + 4."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (y = 2x + 4). The y-intercept is 4 since the line crosses the y-axis there, and the slope is (8-4)/(2-0) = 4/2 = 2, giving y = 2x + 4. Option B swaps the slope and y-intercept values. Option C uses the wrong y-intercept value. Option D uses the wrong slope value.", "image": "question_images/q_352.png"}, {"id": 357, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What is a loop used for in coding?", "options": {"A": "Storing just a single fixed number.", "B": "Checking if a condition is true.", "C": "Repeating a set of instructions automatically.", "D": "Drawing a simple graph or chart."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeating a set of instructions automatically). A loop runs the same steps over and over, usually a fixed number of times or until a condition is met. Option A describes a variable, not a loop. Option B describes an if-statement, a different building block. Option D is not automatically part of what a loop does."}, {"id": 359, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A program sets x to start at 0 and loops while x is less than or equal to 5. Each time through the loop it calculates y = 2x + 4, records the pair (x, y), then increases x by 1. What is the last pair recorded before the loop stops?", "options": {"A": "(5, 14).", "B": "(5, 10).", "C": "(4, 12).", "D": "(6, 16)."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ((5, 14)). The loop keeps running while x is less than or equal to 5, so the last value it uses is x = 5, giving y = 2(5) + 4 = 14. Option D uses x = 6, which is past the point where the loop stops. Option B correctly stops at x = 5 but miscalculates y. Option C uses x = 4, which is one step too early."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "algebra", "question": "In a linear equation, what must be true about the power (exponent) of the variable?", "options": {"A": "It must always be squared to the second power.", "B": "It must be x to the first power, x¹.", "C": "It must be x to the zero power, x⁰.", "D": "It can honestly be raised to any power at all."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It must be x to the first power, x¹): a linear equation only allows the variable to appear to the first power, which is why its graph is a straight line. Squaring the variable (option A) would make the equation quadratic, not linear. A power of 0 (option C) would make the variable term disappear entirely since anything to the power of 0 is 1. Allowing any power (option D) would include curves, which contradicts the definition of linear."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve for x: x + 9 = 16", "options": {"A": "25.", "B": "9.", "C": "-7.", "D": "7."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (7): subtract 9 from both sides of the equation to isolate x, giving x = 16 - 9 = 7. Option A comes from adding instead of subtracting. Option C incorrectly makes the answer negative. Option B mistakenly repeats the number being added instead of solving for x."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve for x: 3x + 4 = x + 14", "options": {"A": "10.", "B": "5.", "C": "9.", "D": "-5."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5): subtract x from both sides to get 2x + 4 = 14, subtract 4 to get 2x = 10, then divide by 2 to get x = 5. Option A comes from not combining the x terms correctly. Option C comes from subtracting 4 instead of adding it back on the wrong side. Option D comes from a sign error when moving terms across the equals sign."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "algebra", "question": "In a table of values, what is a first difference?", "options": {"A": "The amount the y-values change from one row to the next.", "B": "The product of x and y in each row.", "C": "The difference between the first and last y-values only.", "D": "The amount the x-values change each row."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The amount the y-values change from one row to the next): first differences are found by subtracting each y-value from the one right after it. Option D describes the pattern in the x-values, which is a separate check. Option B describes multiplying, not subtracting, which is not how first differences are found. Option C only compares the very first and last values, ignoring every difference in between."}, {"id": 373, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A table of values shows x: 0, 1, 2, 3 and y: 4, 9, 14, 19. What are the first differences, and is the relation linear?", "options": {"A": "5, and the relation is linear.", "B": "5, and the relation is not linear.", "C": "4, and the relation is linear.", "D": "9, and the relation is linear."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5, and the relation is linear): 9 - 4 = 5, 14 - 9 = 5, and 19 - 14 = 5, so every first difference equals 5, and a constant first difference means the relation is linear. Option B correctly finds 5 but wrongly concludes it is not linear, even though a constant difference is exactly the sign of a linear relation. Option C mistakes the y-intercept for the first difference. Option D uses the wrong subtraction and reaches an incorrect number.", "image": "question_images/q_373.png"}, {"id": 375, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What shape does the graph of a linear relation always form?", "options": {"A": "A perfectly straight line.", "B": "A curved parabola shape.", "C": "A smooth curved line.", "D": "A jagged zigzag pattern."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A perfectly straight line): this is the defining feature of a linear relation, and it is exactly what constant first differences guarantee. Option C describes a relation that is not linear. Option B describes the graph of a quadratic relation, not a linear one. Option D describes a relation with no consistent pattern at all."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows a slope triangle drawn on the line y = 3x - 2. Based on the triangle, what is the slope of the line?", "options": {"A": "-2.", "B": "1/3.", "C": "-3.", "D": "3."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3): the slope triangle shows the rise divided by the run between two points on the line, and for this equation that value is 3, matching the coefficient of x. Option A mistakes the y-intercept for the slope. Option B comes from flipping rise and run, giving the reciprocal instead of the true slope. Option C has the correct size but the wrong sign, which would only be true if the line were falling instead of rising.", "image": "question_images/q_379.png"}, {"id": 383, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What kind of slope does a vertical line have?", "options": {"A": "Zero.", "B": "Undefined.", "C": "Positive.", "D": "Negative."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Undefined): a vertical line has a run of zero, and since you cannot divide by zero, the slope is undefined. Option A describes a horizontal line instead, which is the opposite case. Option C and D would both require a run that is not zero, which a vertical line never has."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A table of values shows x: 0, 1, 2, 3 and y: 7, 10, 13, 16. What is the y-intercept of this relation?", "options": {"A": "7.", "B": "3.", "C": "10.", "D": "0."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (7): the y-intercept is the y-value paired with x = 0, and in this table that value is 7. Option B is actually the first difference (the slope) of the relation, not the y-intercept. Option C is the y-value when x = 1, not x = 0. Option D mistakes the x-value at the intercept for the y-value itself."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "algebra", "question": "In the slope-intercept form y = mx + b, what does b represent?", "options": {"A": "The slope.", "B": "The x-intercept.", "C": "The y-intercept.", "D": "The rate of change only."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The y-intercept): b tells you where the line crosses the y-axis, which is also the value of y when x = 0. Option A confuses b with m, which is the slope in this formula. Option B describes a different point entirely, where the line crosses the x-axis. Option D describes m, since the rate of change is the slope, not the y-intercept."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows a line with slope 4 and y-intercept -6. Which equation matches this line?", "options": {"A": "y = -6x + 4.", "B": "y = 4x - 6.", "C": "y = 4x + 6.", "D": "y = -4x - 6."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (y = 4x - 6): substituting m = 4 and b = -6 directly into y = mx + b gives y = 4x - 6. Option A swaps the slope and y-intercept values into the wrong positions. Option C uses the correct slope but the wrong sign on the y-intercept. Option D uses the wrong sign on the slope while keeping the correct y-intercept.", "image": "question_images/q_387.png"}, {"id": 388, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A line passes through the points (0, 6) and (3, 15). What is the equation of the line in slope-intercept form?", "options": {"A": "y = 3x + 6.", "B": "y = 9x + 6.", "C": "y = 3x - 6.", "D": "y = 6x + 3."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (y = 3x + 6): the y-intercept is 6, since (0, 6) has x = 0, and the slope is m = (15 - 6) / (3 - 0) = 9 / 3 = 3, giving y = 3x + 6. Option D swaps the slope and y-intercept into the wrong positions in the equation. Option B forgets to divide the rise by the run when finding the slope. Option C uses the correct slope but the wrong sign on the y-intercept."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A gym charges a $30 sign-up fee plus $15 per month. Write an equation for the total cost C after m months.", "options": {"A": "C = 15m - 30.", "B": "C = 30m + 15.", "C": "C = 45m.", "D": "C = 15m + 30."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (C = 15m + 30): the $30 sign-up fee is paid even before any months pass, so it is the y-intercept, and the $15 per month is the rate of change, so it is the slope, giving C = 15m + 30. Option B swaps the roles of the sign-up fee and the monthly cost. Option C wrongly adds the two numbers together into a single rate instead of keeping a fixed fee separate. Option A uses the wrong sign on the sign-up fee, which should be added, not subtracted."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A phone plan charges a $25 base fee plus $3 per gigabyte (GB) of data used. What is the total cost for a month when 8 GB are used?", "options": {"A": "$28.", "B": "$46.", "C": "$33.", "D": "$49."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($49): using Cost = 3g + 25 with g = 8, Cost = 3(8) + 25 = 24 + 25 = 49. Option A adds the base fee and the per-GB rate together instead of multiplying the rate by the number of GB. Option C uses only 8 dollars of usage cost instead of the full 24 dollars. Option B comes from a multiplication slip when calculating 3 times 8."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The grid shows the points (0,-3), (2,1), and (4,5). What is the slope between the first two labeled points, (0,-3) and (2,1)?", "options": {"A": "4.", "B": "-2.", "C": "2.", "D": "1/2."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (2): using the slope formula, m = (1 - (-3)) / (2 - 0) = 4 / 2 = 2. Option A stops after finding the rise and forgets to divide by the run. Option B mistakes the y-intercept, -3, for part of the slope calculation with the wrong sign. Option D comes from flipping the fraction upside down, dividing run by rise instead of rise by run.", "image": "question_images/q_392.png"}, {"id": 393, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What is the formula for finding the slope between two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2)?", "options": {"A": "m = (y2 + y1) / (x2 + x1).", "B": "m = (x2 - x1) / (y2 - y1).", "C": "m = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1).", "D": "m = (x2 - y1) / (y2 - x1)."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (m = (y2 - y1) / (x2 - x1)): this is the rise (change in y) divided by the run (change in x), which is the definition of slope. Option B flips the fraction upside down, giving the reciprocal of the real slope. Option A adds the coordinates instead of subtracting them, which does not measure change at all. Option D mixes x and y values incorrectly between the numerator and denominator."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A table of values shows x: 0, 2, 4, 6 and y: 3, 7, 11, 15. What is the slope of this relation?", "options": {"A": "4.", "B": "2.", "C": "8.", "D": "1."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (2): the y-value increases by 4 each time, but the x-value increases by 2 each time (not 1), so the slope is 4 / 2 = 2. Option A uses only the change in y and forgets to divide by the change in x. Option C comes from an incorrect multiplication instead of division. Option D comes from a division error using the wrong numbers."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A plain-language program uses an if-statement to check whether the point (5, 8) satisfies y = 3x - 2. What does the program conclude?", "options": {"A": "Yes, the point actually satisfies the relation perfectly, according to this exact equation.", "B": "No, because the x-value of 5 is simply much too large in this case.", "C": "No, the point does not satisfy the relation because the calculated y is 13, not 8.", "D": "Yes, because both 5 and 8 happen to be whole numbers in this case."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, the point does not satisfy the relation because the calculated y is 13, not 8): substituting x = 5 gives y = 3(5) - 2 = 13, which does not match the point's y-value of 8, so the point does not satisfy the relation. Option A wrongly claims the values match when they do not. Option B gives the correct conclusion but for a reason that has nothing to do with checking the equation. Option D also gives an incorrect conclusion based on an irrelevant reason about the numbers being whole."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve for x: 2(x + 5) = 3x + 4", "options": {"A": "3.", "B": "-6.", "C": "14.", "D": "6."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (6): expand the left side to get 2x + 10 = 3x + 4, subtract 2x from both sides to get 10 = x + 4, then subtract 4 to get x = 6. Option A comes from an error while combining the constant terms. Option C forgets to isolate x after combining terms, leaving an intermediate value. Option B comes from a sign error introduced while expanding the brackets."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows the line y = -2x + 8. Based on the graph, what happens to y as x increases?", "options": {"A": "y increases.", "B": "y stays the same.", "C": "y decreases.", "D": "y becomes undefined."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (y decreases): the slope of this line is -2, and a negative slope means the line falls as you move to the right, so y decreases as x increases. Option A describes what happens with a positive slope, which is the opposite of this line. Option B describes a line with a slope of zero, which this line does not have. Option D would only apply to a vertical line, which this equation does not represent.", "image": "question_images/q_403.png"}, {"id": 409, "domain": "algebra", "question": "Solve for x: x/3 + 4 = 9.", "options": {"A": "39.", "B": "1.67.", "C": "5.", "D": "15."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (15): first subtract 4 from both sides to get x/3 = 5, then multiply both sides by 3 to get x = 15. Checking by substitution, 15/3 + 4 = 5 + 4 = 9, which matches. Option C stops one step early without multiplying by 3, option A multiplies before subtracting the 4, and option B mistakenly divides instead of multiplying."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows four points plotted from a table of values: (0, 1), (1, 4), (2, 9), and (3, 16). Based on the first differences of the y-values, which are 3, 5, and 7, is this relation linear?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the points form a clear pattern.", "B": "Yes, because the x-values increase evenly.", "C": "No, because the graph shows a curve, but a curved shape alone cannot prove this.", "D": "No, because the first differences are not constant."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because the first differences are not constant): since the y-values change by 3, then 5, then 7, the differences keep growing instead of staying the same, so the relation is not linear. Option A and option B describe patterns that are not the actual mathematical test for linearity. Option C gives the right conclusion but for the wrong reason, since a curved appearance is not the official test taught in this chapter.", "image": "question_images/q_417.png"}, {"id": 421, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What is the easiest way to graph a linear relation when you are only given its equation?", "options": {"A": "Guess two completely random points anywhere at all on the entire coordinate plane without any real reasoning.", "B": "Graph only the single y-intercept point on the coordinate plane and then simply stop there completely without doing anything else.", "C": "Substitute a few simple x-values, like 0, 1, and 2, to build a small table, then plot and connect the points.", "D": "Rearrange the entire equation into some different algebraic form without ever plotting any points at all."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Substitute a few simple x-values, like 0, 1, and 2, to build a small table, then plot and connect the points): this turns an equation into a set of ordered pairs you can plot with confidence. Option A risks plotting incorrect points with no way to check them. Option B stops after a single point, which cannot show the direction or steepness of the line. Option D never plots anything, so it does not produce a graph at all."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows a line passing through (0, 8), (2, 4), and (4, 0). What is the slope of this line?", "options": {"A": "2.", "B": "-4.", "C": "-2.", "D": "8."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (-2): using two of the points, the slope is (4 - 8)/(2 - 0) = -4/2 = -2, which matches the line falling as it moves right. Option D is the y-intercept, not the slope. Option A drops the negative sign, and option B uses the rise value alone without dividing by the run.", "image": "question_images/q_425.png"}, {"id": 428, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What is true about the y-intercept of a linear relation?", "options": {"A": "It is always going to be some kind of negative number value.", "B": "It is the point where the line crosses the x-axis.", "C": "It is always exactly equal to the line's overall slope value itself.", "D": "It is the point where the line crosses the y-axis, where x = 0."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is the point where the line crosses the y-axis, where x = 0): the y-intercept is found by looking at the y-value when x equals 0. Option A is not always true since y-intercepts can be positive, negative, or zero. Option B describes the x-intercept instead, and option C confuses two completely different values."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A table shows x: 0, 1, 2, 3 and y: 6, 10, 14, 18. What is the equation of this relation in slope-intercept form?", "options": {"A": "y = 6x + 4.", "B": "y = 4x + 6.", "C": "y = 6x - 4.", "D": "y = 4x - 6."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (y = 4x + 6): the first differences are constant at 4, so the slope is m = 4, and the y-value at x = 0 gives the y-intercept b = 6, so the equation is y = 4x + 6. Checking with x = 2 gives y = 4(2) + 6 = 14, which matches the table. Options A and C swap the slope and y-intercept values, and option D has the wrong sign on the y-intercept."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A program sets x to start at 0, then repeats these steps while x is less than or equal to 4: calculate y using y = 3x + 1, record the pair, then increase x by 1. What pair is recorded when x = 3?", "options": {"A": "(3, 9).", "B": "(3, 13).", "C": "(3, 10).", "D": "(4, 13)."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ((3, 10)): substituting x = 3 into y = 3x + 1 gives y = 3(3) + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10, so the recorded pair is (3, 10). Option A forgets to add the 1. Option B uses a value of x that has not been reached yet, and option D records the pair for the next loop step instead of the current one."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "algebra", "question": "The graph shows two points, (2, 5) and (6, 17). What is the slope of the line that connects them?", "options": {"A": "3.", "B": "4.", "C": "12.", "D": "0.33."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3): using m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1), the slope is (17 - 5)/(6 - 2) = 12/4 = 3. Option C forgets to divide by the run. Option B mistakenly uses the run value as the slope, and option D flips the slope fraction upside down.", "image": "question_images/q_442.png"}, {"id": 443, "domain": "algebra", "question": "What is the correct slope between the points (2, 9) and (6, 1)?", "options": {"A": "2.", "B": "-2.", "C": "0.5.", "D": "-0.5."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (-2): using m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1), the slope is (1 - 9)/(6 - 2) = -8/4 = -2, since y decreases as x increases. Option A drops the negative sign that shows the line is falling. Options C and D flip the fraction upside down, which also produces the wrong magnitude."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "algebra", "question": "A line rises 3 units for every 6 units it runs horizontally. A student writes the slope as 6/3 = 2 instead of 3/6. What mistake did the student make?", "options": {"A": "They used the wrong points entirely.", "B": "They forgot to check their answer using substitution.", "C": "They added the rise and run together instead of dividing.", "D": "They wrote run over rise instead of rise over run."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They wrote run over rise instead of rise over run): slope must always be calculated as rise (change in y) divided by run (change in x), and flipping the fraction gives the reciprocal of the real slope. Option A is not the issue since the numbers themselves were not wrong, only their placement. Option B describes a separate good habit that would not have caused this particular mistake, and option C describes a different kind of error that was not made here."}, {"id": 451, "domain": "data", "question": "A survey asks Grade 9 students their favorite video game genre. What type of data is this?", "options": {"A": "Continuous numerical data.", "B": "Discrete numerical data.", "C": "Categorical data.", "D": "A census."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Categorical data): favorite genre describes a quality or category, not an amount, so it is categorical data. You cannot do math on categorical answers like 'racing' or 'puzzle', you can only count how often each one is chosen. It is not numerical, since no number is being measured. It is also not a census or sample type, those describe how data is collected, not what kind of data it is."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "data", "question": "What is the difference between a census and a sample?", "options": {"A": "A census is always completely biased in every situation, while a sample never is at all.", "B": "A sample always contains far more individual people overall than any full census ever does.", "C": "A census collects data from every member of a population, while a sample collects data from only part of it.", "D": "A census only really works properly for categorical types of data and never works for numerical data at all."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A census collects data from every member of a population, while a sample collects data from only part of it): a census means asking everyone in the group you care about, while a sample means asking a smaller portion and using it to make guesses about the whole. A census is not automatically biased, bias comes from how a sample is chosen, not from surveying everyone. A sample is smaller than a census by definition, not larger. Both censuses and samples can be used with categorical or numerical data."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "data", "question": "A student wants to graph how many classmates chose pepperoni, cheese, or mushroom as their favorite pizza topping. Which graph is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Histogram.", "B": "Bar graph.", "C": "Scatter plot.", "D": "Line of best fit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bar graph, because the topping choices are separate categories): pizza topping is categorical data, so separate, non-touching bars correctly represent each distinct choice. A histogram is for continuous data grouped into ranges, which does not apply to topping names. There is only one variable being measured (topping choice), so a scatter plot does not fit. A line of best fit is used to show a trend in two-variable numerical data, not category counts."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "data", "question": "Find the median of this data set: 12, 15, 9, 20, 18, 14.", "options": {"A": "14.", "B": "15.", "C": "12.", "D": "14.5."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (14.5): sorting the data gives 9, 12, 14, 15, 18, 20, and since there are 6 values (an even number), the median is the average of the two middle values, 14 and 15, which is 14.5. 14 alone and 15 alone are each just one of the two middle values, not their average. 12 is not a middle value at all in the sorted list."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "data", "question": "Find the mode(s) of this data set: 3, 5, 5, 7, 8, 8, 9.", "options": {"A": "5 and 8.", "B": "8 only.", "C": "5 only.", "D": "There is no mode."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5 and 8): both 5 and 8 appear exactly twice, more often than any other value, which appear only once, so this data set has two modes. 5 only ignores that 8 also repeats twice. 8 only ignores that 5 also repeats twice. There is a mode here, in fact two of them, so 'no mode' is incorrect."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "data", "question": "Set A has a range of 4 (values 20, 21, 22, 23, 24) and Set B has a range of 40 (values 20, 21, 22, 23, 60), but Set B's values are almost identical to Set A's except for one very high value. What does this tell you about using range alone to measure spread?", "options": {"A": "Range always gives a perfectly exact and complete picture of absolutely every single value's true spread.", "B": "Range is honestly only ever useful for purely categorical types of survey data and nothing else.", "C": "Set B definitely has far more overall variability among most of its individual values than Set A.", "D": "Range can be misleading because a single outlier can make the range look much larger than the data really is."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Range can be misleading because a single outlier can make the range look much larger than the data really is): range only looks at the two extreme values, so a single unusual value like 60 can make the spread look ten times bigger even though most of the data is tightly bunched. Range does not always give an exact picture, since it ignores every value except the max and min. Range is a numerical measure, not something limited to categorical data, which cannot even have a range. Set B's typical values are actually just as bunched together as Set A's, the outlier alone changed the range."}, {"id": 484, "domain": "data", "question": "A scatter plot shows that as the number of hours a plant is watered increases, the plant's height also tends to increase. What kind of correlation is this?", "options": {"A": "Negative correlation.", "B": "No correlation.", "C": "Positive correlation.", "D": "Categorical correlation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Positive correlation): as one variable (watering) increases and the other variable (height) also tends to increase, the points trend upward, which is the definition of positive correlation. Negative correlation would mean height decreases as watering increases, which is the opposite of what is described. No correlation would mean there is no clear pattern at all, which does not match this scenario. 'Categorical correlation' is not a real term used for scatter plot trends."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "data", "question": "Using the table above, what type of correlation exists between hours studied and test score?", "options": {"A": "Positive correlation.", "B": "No correlation.", "C": "Negative correlation.", "D": "Cannot be determined."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Positive correlation): as hours studied increases from 1 to 5, the test score also increases steadily from 65 to 90, showing a clear upward trend. Negative correlation would require scores to decrease as hours studied increases, which is not the case. No correlation would mean the points show no clear pattern, but here the pattern is clearly upward. The trend can be determined from this data, since the scores rise consistently as hours increase.", "image": "question_images/q_485.png"}, {"id": 486, "domain": "data", "question": "A scatter plot compares hours spent watching TV the night before a test with test score: (1,88), (2,82), (3,75), (4,68), (5,60). What kind of correlation does this show?", "options": {"A": "Positive correlation.", "B": "No correlation.", "C": "A census.", "D": "Negative correlation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Negative correlation): as TV hours increase from 1 to 5, the test score steadily decreases from 88 to 60, so the points trend downward, which is negative correlation. Positive correlation would require the score to rise along with TV hours, which is the opposite of this data. No correlation would mean no clear pattern exists, but this data shows a clear downward trend. A census describes a method of data collection, not a correlation type, so it does not belong in this comparison."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "data", "question": "Probability is always written as a number between which two values?", "options": {"A": "-1 and 1.", "B": "1 and 10.", "C": "0 and 1.", "D": "0 and 100."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0 and 1): probability is always between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain), and can also be expressed as a fraction, decimal, or percent. 1 and 10 is not the correct range used for probability at all. -1 and 1 describes the range of correlation strength in some statistics contexts, not basic probability. 0 and 100 describes a percent scale, but probability itself as a decimal or fraction is between 0 and 1, with percent being just one way to express it."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "data", "question": "What is a 'truncated axis' on a graph?", "options": {"A": "An axis with a scale that skips numbers unevenly.", "B": "An axis drawn in 3D to look more visual.", "C": "An axis that shows only categorical data.", "D": "A vertical axis that does not start at zero."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A vertical axis that does not start at zero): a truncated axis cuts off the lower part of the scale, which can make small real differences between bars look much bigger than they actually are. An axis with unevenly skipping numbers describes an inconsistent scale, a related but different trick. An axis drawn in 3D describes the 3D visual exaggeration trick, not truncation. A truncated axis is about where the scale starts, not about whether the data is categorical or numerical."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "data", "question": "The table above shows how many pets 25 students own. How many students own 2 or more pets?", "options": {"A": "7.", "B": "9.", "C": "10.", "D": "3."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (10): students owning 2 or more pets includes the 7 students who own exactly 2 and the 3 students who own exactly 3, so 7 + 3 = 10. Option A (7) only counts students with exactly 2 pets, and option D (3) only counts students with exactly 3 pets, both ignoring the other group. Option B (9) is the frequency for students who own exactly 1 pet, which does not meet the 'or more' condition. Adding the two highest frequency rows together gives the correct total of 10 students.", "image": "question_images/q_503.png"}, {"id": 504, "domain": "data", "question": "A student wants to find the favorite lunch food of the whole school but only surveys players on the football team. What is the biggest problem with this sample?", "options": {"A": "The sample size chosen is simply far too large.", "B": "The data that was collected here is continuous, not categorical.", "C": "It actually uses a full census instead of just a sample.", "D": "The sample is biased and does not represent the whole school."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The sample is biased and does not represent the whole school): only asking football players leaves out every other student, so the results are skewed toward one group's tastes. The sample size being too large is not the issue here, since the sample is actually too narrow, not too big. Favorite food is categorical data no matter who is asked, so option B is incorrect. This is a sample, not a census, because it does not include every member of the population, ruling out option C."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "data", "question": "A tally chart shows 4 full bundles of five tally marks plus 2 extra marks for the value 'blue' in a favorite color survey. What is the frequency of blue?", "options": {"A": "18.", "B": "20.", "C": "22.", "D": "24."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (22): each full bundle represents 5 tally marks, so 4 bundles equal 4 times 5, which is 20, plus the 2 extra marks gives 20 + 2 = 22. Option B (20) forgets to add the 2 extra marks left over after the bundles. Option A (18) incorrectly subtracts instead of adding the extra marks. Option D (24) mistakenly adds 4 extra marks instead of the 2 that were actually shown, so it overcounts the total."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "data", "question": "What is the key visual difference between a bar graph and a histogram?", "options": {"A": "Histogram bars touch because the data is continuous, but bar graph bars have gaps because the categories are separate.", "B": "Bar graphs actually use small circles while histograms instead always use straight rectangular bars only.", "C": "Bar graphs are strictly only used for probability, and histograms are strictly only used for surveys.", "D": "Histograms can honestly only ever show exactly two categories at any one single time."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Histogram bars touch because the data is continuous, but bar graph bars have gaps because the categories are separate): histograms group continuous numerical data into ranges, so the bars are drawn touching to show the data flows smoothly from one interval to the next. Bar graphs represent categorical or discrete data, where each bar stands alone, so gaps are used between bars. Option B is false since both graphs use rectangular bars, not circles. Option C and D describe made-up limitations that have nothing to do with how these two graphs actually work."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "data", "question": "In a survey of 50 students, 15 chose swimming as their favorite summer activity. What percent of the students chose swimming?", "options": {"A": "15%.", "B": "30%.", "C": "50%.", "D": "35%."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (30%): dividing the favorable count by the total, 15 divided by 50 equals 0.3, and multiplying by 100 gives 30%. Option A (15%) mistakes the raw count of students for a percent instead of dividing it by the total. Option C (50%) is simply the total number of students surveyed, not a percent at all. Option D (35%) does not match the actual division and is not supported by the numbers given in the question."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "data", "question": "Find the median of these six numbers: 12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 30.", "options": {"A": "19.", "B": "18.", "C": "20.", "D": "21."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (19): since there are 6 values, an even number, the median is the mean of the two middle values, which are 18 and 20, so (18 + 20) divided by 2 equals 19. Option B (18) and option C (20) are each only one of the two middle values on their own, not their average. Option D (21) does not match any correct calculation from this data set. Whenever a data set has an even count, always average the two middle numbers instead of picking just one."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "data", "question": "Umbrella sales and the number of car accidents both increase on rainy days. What does this best illustrate?", "options": {"A": "Umbrella sales somehow directly cause more car accidents.", "B": "Car accidents somehow directly cause more umbrella sales.", "C": "There is absolutely no relationship at all between the two.", "D": "Correlation does not always mean causation, since rain likely causes both."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Correlation does not always mean causation, since rain likely causes both): umbrella sales and car accidents rise together only because rainy weather independently causes both, not because one causes the other. Option A and option B both wrongly assume a direct cause and effect link between two things that are only connected through a third factor, the weather. Option C is wrong because there clearly is a relationship, a correlation, even though it is not a causal one. This is the classic warning that a strong correlation between two variables does not prove that one directly causes the other."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "data", "question": "How is theoretical probability calculated?", "options": {"A": "Number of trials divided by number of successes.", "B": "Number of times an event occurred divided by total trials.", "C": "Largest value minus smallest value.", "D": "Number of favorable outcomes divided by total possible outcomes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Number of favorable outcomes divided by total possible outcomes): theoretical probability is worked out using logic before anything actually happens, by comparing favorable outcomes to all possible outcomes. Option A reverses the correct ratio and does not describe any real probability formula. Option B describes experimental probability instead, which relies on actual recorded trials. Option C describes how to calculate range, which has nothing to do with probability."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "data", "question": "A coin is flipped 50 times and lands on heads 22 times. What is the experimental probability of getting heads?", "options": {"A": "11/25.", "B": "14/25.", "C": "22/28.", "D": "1/2."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (11/25): experimental probability is the number of times the event actually happened divided by the total number of trials, so 22 out of 50 gives 22/50, which simplifies to 11/25. Option D is the theoretical probability of heads, not what the experiment showed. Option B is the probability of tails, since the coin landed tails 28 times."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "data", "question": "A basketball player attempted 40 free throws and made 28 of them. What is the experimental probability that she makes a free throw, as a percent?", "options": {"A": "28%.", "B": "70%.", "C": "40%.", "D": "12%."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (70%): dividing the number of successful free throws by the total attempts gives 28 divided by 40, which equals 0.7, or 70% when converted to a percent. Option A (28%) mistakes the raw number of successful shots for a percent. Option C (40%) mistakes the total number of attempts for a percent. Option D (12%) comes from incorrectly subtracting instead of dividing, and does not match the real calculation."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "data", "question": "A coin is flipped 20 times and lands on heads 13 times. The theoretical probability of heads is 1/2, or 50%. What is the best explanation for the difference between the experimental and theoretical results?", "options": {"A": "The coin that was used here is definitely unfair and heavily biased toward landing on heads.", "B": "The formula for calculating probability here was almost certainly used incorrectly by mistake.", "C": "Experimental results should always exactly and perfectly match theoretical results every single time without fail.", "D": "With a small number of trials, experimental probability often differs somewhat from theoretical probability, and this is normal."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (With a small number of trials, experimental probability often differs somewhat from theoretical probability, and this is normal): with only 20 flips, some random variation from the expected 50% is completely expected and does not prove anything is wrong. Option A jumps to an unsupported conclusion, since a small sample naturally varies without the coin being unfair. Option B is incorrect, since the experimental probability of 13 divided by 20 was calculated correctly. Option C is false, since experimental results rarely match theoretical results perfectly, especially with fewer trials, and the gap tends to shrink as more trials are performed."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "data", "question": "A bar graph's vertical axis starts at 40 instead of 0. What effect does this have on how the graph looks?", "options": {"A": "It makes small real differences between bars look much bigger than they are.", "B": "It makes all the bars look exactly the same height.", "C": "It automatically fixes any bias in the data.", "D": "It has no effect on how the reader interprets the graph."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It makes small real differences between bars look much bigger than they are): cutting off the bottom of the axis removes visual space, so bars that are actually close in value appear far more different in height. Option B is incorrect because a truncated axis exaggerates differences rather than erasing them. Option C is wrong since a truncated axis is itself a form of misleading presentation, not a fix for bias. Option D is false, since checking where an axis starts is one of the most important steps in reading a graph honestly."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "data", "question": "The table above shows weekly allowance for 5 students. Which measure of central tendency best represents a typical student's allowance, and why?", "options": {"A": "The median of $14.", "B": "The mode.", "C": "The mean of $28.80.", "D": "The range of $78."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The median of $14, because the $90 outlier pulls the mean too high): sorting the values gives 12, 13, 14, 15, 90, so the middle value is 14, which fairly represents four of the five students. Option C is tempting since the mean does use every value, but that is exactly why Eli's unusual $90 allowance drags the mean up to $28.80, far above what most students actually receive. Option B is wrong because none of the allowances repeat, so there is no mode. Option D describes the spread of the data, not a typical value, and is also heavily affected by the same outlier.", "image": "question_images/q_531.png"}, {"id": 540, "domain": "data", "question": "A researcher measures the exact height in centimeters of 200 plants and wants to group the results into ranges like 10-19 cm, 20-29 cm, and 30-39 cm. Which graph should be used?", "options": {"A": "Circle-graph.", "B": "Bar-graph.", "C": "Histogram.", "D": "Scatter-plot."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Histogram): plant height is continuous numerical data, and grouping it into equal ranges with touching bars is exactly what a histogram is built for. Option A is meant for showing parts of a whole as percentages, not grouped measurement data. Option B is used for categorical or discrete data with separate bars, not continuous measurements. Option D is used to compare two different variables at once, but only one variable, height, is being studied here."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "data", "question": "A survey about favorite school subject asks only the 6 students in the chess club. Which change would most improve the sample?", "options": {"A": "Randomly selecting students from many different clubs and grades across the school.", "B": "Only asking chess club members who are specifically in grade 9 only.", "C": "Making the survey questions much harder for anyone to understand.", "D": "Asking that exact same group of 6 students the question twice."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Randomly selecting students from many different clubs and grades across the school): a wider, more random selection reduces bias and gives a more accurate picture of the whole school's opinions. Option C would make the survey worse by confusing respondents, not more accurate. Option B narrows the sample even further instead of broadening it, keeping the same underlying bias. Option D does not add any new information since it simply repeats the same limited group rather than reaching different students."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "data", "question": "A graph's vertical axis jumps from 0 to 10 to 50 to 100, but each jump is drawn with the same amount of space between them. What problem does this cause?", "options": {"A": "It always makes the axis start exactly at zero, which is therefore always perfectly accurate.", "B": "It distorts how steep or flat the trend actually looks, since the spacing does not match the real number gaps.", "C": "It genuinely has no effect at all since every single axis works exactly this same way.", "D": "It automatically and instantly converts absolutely all of the underlying data into percentages instead."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It distorts how steep or flat the trend actually looks, since the spacing does not match the real number gaps): equal visual spacing between unequal number gaps, like 0 to 10 versus 50 to 100, makes changes look more or less dramatic than they really are. Option A is misleading, since starting at zero is good practice, but that alone does not fix an inconsistent scale problem. Option C is false, since properly built axes use consistent, evenly spaced intervals that match the real number gaps. Option D is unrelated, since changing the axis spacing does not convert any values into percentages."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "data", "question": "A student wants to know the favorite weekend activity of the whole school, so she only asks members of the chess club. What is the main problem with her method?", "options": {"A": "The sample size that she personally chose is simply far too large overall.", "B": "She used a full official census instead of just a proper smaller sample.", "C": "The sample is biased because chess club members may not represent the whole school.", "D": "Categorical data of this particular kind simply cannot ever be surveyed at all."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The sample is biased because chess club members may not represent the whole school): chess club members likely share similar interests, so their answers may not reflect what the rest of the school actually prefers. The sample size is small, not too large, so option A is incorrect. She used a sample, not a census, since she only asked part of the population, and categorical data like favorite activity can absolutely be surveyed, so D is also wrong."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "data", "question": "Which graph is best for showing the number of students who chose each favorite ice cream flavor?", "options": {"A": "Histogram.", "B": "Bar graph.", "C": "Scatter plot.", "D": "Line of best fit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bar graph): a bar graph uses separate, unconnected bars, which is ideal for showing categorical data like favorite ice cream flavors. A histogram is used for continuous numerical data grouped into ranges, not categories, so A is wrong. A scatter plot compares two variables and a line of best fit shows a trend in two-variable data, neither of which applies to counting one category, so C and D are also incorrect."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "data", "question": "Why do the bars in a histogram touch each other, unlike the bars in a bar graph?", "options": {"A": "Because histograms only use categorical data.", "B": "Because the data is continuous and grouped into ranges.", "C": "Because there are always exactly four bars.", "D": "Because histograms cannot have gaps by definition."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Because the data is continuous and grouped into ranges): since continuous data flows smoothly from one interval to the next, the bars touch to show that connection with no gaps. Histograms are used for numerical continuous data, not categorical data, so A is incorrect. There is no rule that histograms simply cannot have gaps for no reason, and the number of bars depends on how many intervals are used, not a fixed count of four, so D and C are also wrong."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "data", "question": "What is another common name for the mean of a data set?", "options": {"A": "The middle value.", "B": "The most frequent value.", "C": "The range.", "D": "The average."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The average): the mean is calculated by adding all the values and dividing by how many there are, which is exactly what most people call the average. The middle value describes the median, not the mean, so A is incorrect. The most frequent value describes the mode, and the range measures spread rather than a central value, so B and C are also wrong."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "data", "question": "Find the median of this data set: 14, 22, 9, 31, 18.", "options": {"A": "14.", "B": "22.", "C": "18.", "D": "19."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (18): sorting the values gives 9, 14, 18, 22, 31, and with five values the middle one is the third, which is 18. 14 is the second value in the sorted list, not the middle one, so A is incorrect. 22 is the fourth value, not the middle, and 19 is not even part of the data set at all, so B and D are also wrong."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "data", "question": "Find the median of this data set: 6, 10, 14, 18, 22, 26.", "options": {"A": "14.", "B": "16.", "C": "18.", "D": "15."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (16): with six values already in order, the median is the mean of the two middle numbers, 14 and 18, so (14 + 18) divided by 2 equals 16. 14 and 18 are each only one of the two middle values, not their average, so A and C are incomplete. 15 is not the correct midpoint of 14 and 18, so D is also wrong."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "data", "question": "What is the mode of this data set: 5, 8, 8, 3, 8, 9, 3?", "options": {"A": "8.", "B": "3.", "C": "5.", "D": "9."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (8): the value 8 appears three times in the data set, which is more often than any other value. The value 3 appears twice, which is more than once but still less often than 8, so B is incorrect. The values 5 and 9 each appear only one time, which is not enough to be the mode, so C and D are also wrong."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "data", "question": "A scatter plot shows the pattern (2, 50), (4, 60), (6, 70), (8, 80), (10, 90), where each 2-hour increase adds 10 to the value. Using this pattern, predict the value at x = 12.", "options": {"A": "100.", "B": "95.", "C": "90.", "D": "110."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (100): following the pattern, each increase of 2 in x adds 10 to the value, so moving from x = 10 (value 90) to x = 12 adds another 10, giving 100. 90 is the value already given for x = 10, not the predicted value for x = 12, so C is incorrect. 95 does not match the consistent pattern of adding exactly 10 for every 2-hour increase, and 110 would require adding 20 instead of 10, so B and D are both wrong."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "data", "question": "What is the formula for theoretical probability?", "options": {"A": "times the event occurred divided by total trials.", "B": "total outcomes divided by favorable outcomes.", "C": "frequency divided by range.", "D": "favorable outcomes divided by total possible outcomes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (favorable outcomes divided by total possible outcomes): theoretical probability is calculated using logic before anything happens, by dividing the number of favorable outcomes by the total number of possible outcomes. Times the event occurred divided by total trials is actually the formula for experimental probability, not theoretical, so A describes the wrong concept. Total outcomes divided by favorable outcomes flips the fraction upside down, and frequency divided by range is not a real probability formula at all, so B and C are also incorrect."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "data", "question": "A spinner has 8 equal sections: 3 red, 2 blue, 2 green, 1 yellow. What is the theoretical probability of landing on blue?", "options": {"A": "3/8.", "B": "1/8.", "C": "1/4.", "D": "1/2."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (1/4): there are 2 blue sections out of 8 total sections, so 2/8 simplifies to 1/4. 1/8 would be the probability of landing on the single yellow section, not blue, so B is incorrect. 3/8 is actually the probability of landing on red, since there are 3 red sections, and 1/2 is far too high for only 2 out of 8 sections, so A and D are also wrong."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "data", "question": "A number cube is rolled 60 times and lands on 6 exactly 7 times, while the theoretical probability of rolling a 6 is about 16.7%. What does this experimental result suggest?", "options": {"A": "The number cube that was used here is definitely and completely broken beyond repair.", "B": "Experimental results can differ from theoretical results, especially with a limited number of trials.", "C": "Rolling a 6 is now therefore completely and permanently impossible forever after this.", "D": "The theoretical probability must therefore be entirely recalculated again from scratch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Experimental results can differ from theoretical results, especially with a limited number of trials): 7 out of 60 rolls is about 11.7%, which is lower than the theoretical 16.7%, but this kind of gap is normal and expected, especially without a very large number of trials. A single lower-than-expected result does not prove the number cube is broken, so A jumps to an unsupported conclusion. Theoretical probability is based on the six equally likely sides of the cube and does not change based on one set of experimental results, so D is incorrect, and rolling a 6 clearly still happened, so C is also false."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "data", "question": "Which value could NOT represent a probability?", "options": {"A": "0.", "B": "1.5.", "C": "0.75.", "D": "1."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1.5): probability is always written as a number between 0 and 1, so any value greater than 1, like 1.5, cannot represent a real probability. 0 represents an impossible event and 1 represents a certain event, both of which are valid probability values, so A and D are incorrect choices. 0.75 falls between 0 and 1, which makes it a perfectly valid probability, so C is also not the answer."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "data", "question": "A bar graph's vertical axis starts at 40 instead of 0. What effect does this have on the graph?", "options": {"A": "It has no effect on how the graph looks.", "B": "It automatically and instantly doubles every single value shown.", "C": "It makes small differences between bars look larger than they really are.", "D": "It makes the entire graph nearly impossible for anyone to read."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It makes small differences between bars look larger than they really are): a truncated axis that starts at 40 instead of 0 cuts out the lower portion of the graph, which visually exaggerates small real differences between the bars. This trick absolutely does have an effect on how the graph looks, so A is incorrect. The actual data values are not changed or doubled by shifting where the axis starts, and the graph is still readable, just misleading, so B and D are both wrong."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "data", "question": "A tally chart shows frequencies of 6, 9, 4, and 8 for four categories, and the person collecting the data says there were 25 total responses. What should you check?", "options": {"A": "Whether the mode identified from the tally chart is actually correct.", "B": "Whether 6 + 9 + 4 + 8 actually equals 25.", "C": "Whether the range of 25 matches the highest value minus the lowest value.", "D": "Whether the data type should actually be classified as categorical or numerical."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Whether 6 + 9 + 4 + 8 actually equals 25): adding these frequencies gives 27, not 25, so there is a counting error somewhere in the tally chart that needs to be found. The mode is not directly connected to whether the total count is correct, so A does not address the real issue here. Range measures spread between values, not a total response count, and whether the data is categorical or numerical does not explain a mismatched total, so C and D are also incorrect."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "data", "question": "Using the table above, how many students took the quiz in total?", "options": {"A": "20.", "B": "18.", "C": "19.", "D": "21."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (20): adding all the frequencies in the table, 3 + 5 + 9 + 2 + 1, gives a total of 20 students. 18 and 19 are both slightly too low and do not match the actual sum of the frequency column, so B and C are incorrect. 21 is slightly too high compared to the real total, so D is also wrong.", "image": "question_images/q_587.png"}, {"id": 588, "domain": "data", "question": "Using the table above, what is the mode of the quiz scores?", "options": {"A": "6.", "B": "7.", "C": "8.", "D": "9."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (8): the score of 8 has the highest frequency in the table, with 9 students earning that score, which is more than any other score. The score of 6 only has a frequency of 3, and 7 only has a frequency of 5, both lower than 8's frequency of 9, so A and B are incorrect. The score of 9 has a frequency of only 2, which is far from being the most common score, so D is also wrong.", "image": "question_images/q_588.png"}, {"id": 593, "domain": "data", "question": "A researcher wants to find the average commute time for all employees at a large company, so she only surveys employees who arrive before 7:00 am. What is the main issue with her sample?", "options": {"A": "It is far too large in size to be considered useful for any meaningful statistical analysis.", "B": "It is likely biased, since early arrivers may have different commutes than other employees.", "C": "It is technically considered a full census of the population rather than a smaller sample.", "D": "It uses continuous numerical data incorrectly instead of the correct categorical data type here."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is likely biased, since early arrivers may have different commutes than other employees): employees who arrive very early might have shorter commutes or different schedules than the rest of the staff, so this group does not fairly represent everyone. Nothing about the description suggests the sample is too large, so A is not the real problem here. Only surveying part of the employees makes this a sample, not a census, and commute time being continuous data is not being used incorrectly, so C and D are also wrong."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "data", "question": "Using the table above, what is the probability of NOT picking a Red counter?", "options": {"A": "3/5.", "B": "2/5.", "C": "1/2.", "D": "1/3."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3/5): there are 30 counters total and 12 are red, so 18 counters are not red, and 18/30 simplifies to 3/5. 2/5 would actually be close to the probability of picking red itself (12/30 simplifies to 2/5), which is the opposite of what is being asked, so B is incorrect. 1/2 and 1/3 do not match the simplified form of 18/30, so C and D are also wrong.", "image": "question_images/q_595.png"}, {"id": 602, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A wire measures 3200 mm long. What is this length in metres?", "options": {"A": "3.2 m.", "B": "32 m.", "C": "0.32 m.", "D": "320 m."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3.2 m): since 1 mm equals 1/1000 of a metre, divide 3200 by 1000 to get 3.2 m. Option B divides by only 100, the conversion factor for cm to m, not mm to m. Option C divides by an extra factor of 10 too many. Option D divides by only 10, the wrong power of ten for a millimetre to metre conversion."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangular flag has sides measuring 5 cm, 6 cm, and 7 cm. What is the perimeter of the flag?", "options": {"A": "11 cm.", "B": "210 cm.", "C": "18 cm.", "D": "19 cm."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (18 cm): for a triangle you add all three side lengths, so 5 plus 6 plus 7 equals 18 cm. Option A only adds two of the three sides and forgets the third. Option B multiplies the three side lengths together instead of adding them. Option D makes a simple addition slip when combining the three sides.", "image": "question_images/q_607.png"}, {"id": 611, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular poster has a length of 12 cm and a width of 7 cm. What is the area of the poster?", "options": {"A": "84 cm squared.", "B": "19 cm squared.", "C": "38 cm squared.", "D": "168 cm squared."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (84 cm squared): the area of a rectangle is length times width, so 12 times 7 equals 84 cm squared. Option C calculates the perimeter instead of the area. Option B adds the length and width instead of multiplying them. Option D doubles the correct area unnecessarily.", "image": "question_images/q_611.png"}, {"id": 616, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A circular clock face has a diameter of 10 cm. What is the area of the clock face? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "314 cm squared.", "B": "78.5 cm squared.", "C": "19.625 cm squared.", "D": "31.4 cm squared."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (78.5 cm squared): the area formula always needs the radius, so first find the radius by dividing the diameter by 2, giving 5 cm, then compute 3.14 times 5 squared equals 3.14 times 25, equals 78.5 cm squared. Option A mistakenly plugs the diameter directly into the area formula instead of the radius. Option D actually calculates the circumference, not the area. Option C uses one quarter of the diameter instead of one half."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangular prism has triangular ends with a base of 6 cm, a height of 4 cm, and three sides of 5 cm, 5 cm, and 6 cm. The prism is 10 cm long. What is the surface area of the prism?", "options": {"A": "184 cm squared.", "B": "264 cm squared.", "C": "24 cm squared.", "D": "172 cm squared."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (184 cm squared): the triangular ends have area one half times 6 times 4 equals 12 cm squared each, and the perimeter of the triangle is 5 plus 5 plus 6 equals 16 cm, so surface area equals 2 times 12, plus 16 times 10, equals 24 plus 160, equals 184 cm squared. Option D forgets to double the triangle end area before adding the rectangular sides. Option B mistakenly uses the triangle's base times height instead of its perimeter for the rectangular side area. Option C only counts the two triangular ends and forgets the three rectangular sides completely.", "image": "question_images/q_620.png"}, {"id": 624, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A sphere has a radius of 5 cm. What is its surface area? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "78.5 cm squared.", "B": "157 cm squared.", "C": "523.33 cm squared.", "D": "314 cm squared."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (314 cm squared): a sphere's surface area is 4 times pi times r squared, so 4 times 3.14 times 25 equals 4 times 78.5, equals 314 cm squared. Option A forgets the factor of 4 entirely and only calculates pi times r squared. Option B uses a factor of 2 instead of 4. Option C is actually the sphere's volume, not its surface area.", "image": "question_images/q_624.png"}, {"id": 625, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular box has length 6 cm, width 3 cm, and height 2 cm. What is the total surface area of the box, counting every face?", "options": {"A": "66 cm squared.", "B": "60 cm squared.", "C": "72 cm squared.", "D": "36 cm squared."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (72 cm squared): using SA equals 2 times (lw plus lh plus wh), 2 times (18 plus 12 plus 6) equals 2 times 36, equals 72 cm squared. Option B forgets to include one whole matching pair of faces, the two smallest ends. Option A only forgets a single face instead of realizing faces come in matching pairs. Option D is actually the volume of the prism, not its surface area.", "image": "question_images/q_625.png"}, {"id": 631, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylinder has a radius of 5 cm and a height of 6 cm. What is its volume? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "94.2 cm cubed.", "B": "471 cm cubed.", "C": "188.4 cm cubed.", "D": "78.5 cm cubed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (471 cm cubed): volume is pi times r squared times h, so 3.14 times 25 times 6 equals 471 cm cubed. Option A forgets to square the radius and just multiplies pi by the radius once. Option C uses the circumference formula, 2 times pi times r, multiplied by the height, instead of the area formula. Option D calculates the circle's base area but forgets to multiply by the height.", "image": "question_images/q_631.png"}, {"id": 638, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylinder has a diameter of 8 cm and a height of 5 cm. What is the volume of the cylinder? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "1004.8 cm cubed.", "B": "50.24 cm cubed.", "C": "62.8 cm cubed.", "D": "251.2 cm cubed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (251.2 cm cubed): since the radius is half the diameter, the radius here is 8 divided by 2, equals 4 cm, so volume equals 3.14 times 16 times 5, equals 251.2 cm cubed. Option A mistakenly uses the diameter of 8 cm directly as the radius instead of halving it first. Option C forgets to square the radius before multiplying. Option B correctly finds the radius but forgets to multiply by the height.", "image": "question_images/q_638.png"}, {"id": 639, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular yard needs exactly 24 m of fencing around it. Which of these rectangles, all using the full 24 m of fencing, has the largest possible area?", "options": {"A": "10 m by 2 m.", "B": "8 m by 4 m.", "C": "9 m by 3 m.", "D": "6 m by 6 m."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (6 m by 6 m): with a fixed perimeter of 24 m, the square shape always gives the maximum area, and checking confirms 6 times 6 equals 36 m squared, larger than any other option. Option A, 10 m by 2 m, gives only 20 m squared because it is the most stretched out shape. Option B, 8 m by 4 m, gives 32 m squared, better but still less than the square. Option C, 9 m by 3 m, gives 27 m squared, also less than the square."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangle must have an area of exactly 36 m squared. Which of these rectangles, all with that same area, needs the least amount of fencing around its perimeter?", "options": {"A": "6 m by 6 m.", "B": "9 m by 4 m.", "C": "18 m by 2 m.", "D": "12 m by 3 m."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (6 m by 6 m): with a fixed area of 36 m squared, the square always needs the least perimeter, and 6 times 6 equals 36 m squared with a perimeter of 2 times (6 plus 6) equals 24 m, the smallest of all the choices. Option D needs 30 m of perimeter for the same area. Option B needs 26 m of perimeter. Option C needs 40 m of perimeter, the most stretched out and least efficient shape listed."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "For a fixed perimeter, which rectangle always encloses the maximum possible area?", "options": {"A": "No difference.", "B": "A long rectangle.", "C": "A thin rectangle.", "D": "a square."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (a square): when the perimeter is fixed, the square is always the most efficient shape and encloses the largest possible area among all rectangles. Option B describes a rectangle that is less efficient than a square and will always enclose a smaller area for the same perimeter. Option C describes the least efficient shape, since stretching a rectangle out while keeping the same perimeter shrinks its area. Option A is false, since rectangles with the same perimeter can have very different areas depending on their shape."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A closed rectangular box has length 7 cm, width 5 cm, and height 3 cm. A packaging designer accidentally forgets to include the bottom face when calculating how much cardboard is needed. How much cardboard is missing from the designer's total?", "options": {"A": "35 cm squared.", "B": "24 cm squared.", "C": "15 cm squared.", "D": "70 cm squared."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (35 cm squared): the missing bottom face is one of the length by width faces, so its area is 7 times 5 equals 35 cm squared. Option D mistakenly doubles this, as if both the top and bottom faces were missing instead of just one. Option B uses the perimeter of that face instead of its area. Option C calculates the area of a different face, width times height, instead of the correct length by width bottom face.", "image": "question_images/q_644.png"}, {"id": 646, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A piece of ribbon measures 6.2 m long. How many centimetres long is the ribbon?", "options": {"A": "6200 cm.", "B": "62 cm.", "C": "620 cm.", "D": "0.62 cm."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (620 cm): To convert metres to centimetres you multiply by 100, since there are 100 cm in 1 m. 6.2 times 100 equals 620 cm. Option B multiplies by only 10 instead of 100, option A multiplies by 1000 as if converting from km, and option D divides by 10 by mistake."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A wire is measured as 4700 mm long. What is this length in metres?", "options": {"A": "47 m.", "B": "470 m.", "C": "4.7 m.", "D": "0.47 m."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (4.7 m): To convert millimetres to metres you divide by 1000, since there are 1000 mm in 1 m. 4700 divided by 1000 equals 4.7 m. Option A divides by only 100 as if converting to cm, option B divides by only 10, the wrong power of ten, and option D divides by 10000 by mistake."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A garden plot has an area of 3.6 square metres. What is this area in square centimetres?", "options": {"A": "360 cm².", "B": "36000 cm².", "C": "3600 cm².", "D": "360000 cm²."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (36000 cm²): Since 1 m squared equals 10000 cm squared (100 squared, not just 100), you multiply 3.6 by 10000 to get 36000 cm². Option A uses the plain length conversion factor of 100 instead of squaring it, option C uses 1000, and option D multiplies by 100000 by mistake."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Which formula correctly finds the circumference of a circle when you are given the radius?", "options": {"A": "C = pi × r².", "B": "C = pi × r.", "C": "C = 2 × pi × r.", "D": "C = 4 × pi × r."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (C = 2 × pi × r): Since diameter equals 2 times the radius, substituting into C = pi times d gives C = 2 times pi times r. Option A is the area formula for a circle, option B forgets to double the radius, and option D confuses the circumference formula with a sphere calculation."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular poster measures 14 cm by 9 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is the area of the poster?", "options": {"A": "46 cm².", "B": "63 cm².", "C": "126 cm².", "D": "23 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (126 cm²): The area of a rectangle is length times width, so 14 times 9 equals 126 cm². Option A (46 cm²) is actually the poster's perimeter, not its area. Option B is half the correct area, and option D is simply length plus width (23) rather than length times width.", "image": "question_images/q_656.png"}, {"id": 658, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A parallelogram-shaped tile has a base of 12 cm, a perpendicular height of 5 cm, and a slanted side of 7 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is the area of the tile?", "options": {"A": "60 cm².", "B": "84 cm².", "C": "17 cm².", "D": "30 cm²."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (60 cm²): The area of a parallelogram is base times height, using the perpendicular height, so 12 times 5 equals 60 cm². Option B mistakenly multiplies the base by the slanted side length (7 cm) instead of the true height, option C simply adds base and height instead of multiplying, and option D incorrectly halves the correct area as if it were a triangle.", "image": "question_images/q_658.png"}, {"id": 662, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A square piece of metal measures 14 cm by 14 cm, with a circular hole of radius 4 cm cut from the centre, as shown in the diagram. What is the remaining area of the metal?", "options": {"A": "50.24 cm².", "B": "196 cm².", "C": "145.76 cm².", "D": "246.24 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (145.76 cm²): The square's area is 14 times 14, equals 196 cm², and the circular hole's area is pi times 4 squared, equals 3.14 times 16, equals 50.24 cm², so the remaining area is 196 minus 50.24, equals 145.76 cm². Option B is only the square with no hole subtracted, option A is only the circular hole itself, and option D mistakenly adds the two areas together instead of subtracting.", "image": "question_images/q_662.png"}, {"id": 663, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular prism has length 6 cm, width 5 cm, and height 4 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is its total surface area?", "options": {"A": "148 cm².", "B": "120 cm².", "C": "74 cm².", "D": "176 cm²."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (148 cm²): The surface area formula is 2 times (lw plus lh plus wh), so 2 times (30 plus 24 plus 20), equals 2 times 74, equals 148 cm². Option C forgets to multiply the sum of the three face areas by 2. Option B is actually the volume of the prism (6 times 5 times 4, equals 120), not a surface area. Option D comes from adding the wrong combination of face areas instead of following the formula exactly.", "image": "question_images/q_663.png"}, {"id": 665, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylindrical can has a radius of 5 cm and a height of 12 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is its total surface area? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "376.8 cm².", "B": "157 cm².", "C": "533.8 cm².", "D": "470.4 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (533.8 cm²): SA equals 2 times pi times r squared, plus 2 times pi times r times h, so 2 times 3.14 times 25, plus 2 times 3.14 times 5 times 12, equals 157 plus 376.8, equals 533.8 cm². Option A is only the curved side area with the two circular ends left out. Option B is only the area of the two circular ends with the curved side left out, and option D comes from mismatching the two parts of the formula.", "image": "question_images/q_665.png"}, {"id": 667, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A square-based pyramid has a base side of 8 cm and a slant height of 9 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is its total surface area?", "options": {"A": "144 cm².", "B": "272 cm².", "C": "100 cm².", "D": "208 cm²."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (208 cm²): The base area is 8 times 8, equals 64 cm², and the four triangular faces total 4 times (one half times 8 times 9), equals 4 times 36, equals 144 cm², so SA equals 64 plus 144, equals 208 cm². Option A only counts the four triangular faces and leaves out the square base. Option C mistakenly uses a smaller triangle area, and option B mistakenly counts the square base twice, giving 64 plus 64 plus 144, equals 272 cm².", "image": "question_images/q_667.png"}, {"id": 671, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylindrical pipe has a radius of 4 cm and a length of 15 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is its volume?", "options": {"A": "753.6 cm³.", "B": "376.8 cm³.", "C": "188.4 cm³.", "D": "3014.4 cm³."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (753.6 cm³): Volume equals pi times r squared times h, so 3.14 times 16 times 15 equals 753.6 cm³. Option B is half the correct volume. Option C forgets to square the radius, computing 3.14 times 4 times 15 instead, and option D mistakenly uses the diameter squared instead of the radius squared, giving 3.14 times 64 times 15.", "image": "question_images/q_671.png"}, {"id": 680, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A square has an area of 5 square metres. What is this area in square centimetres?", "options": {"A": "500 cm².", "B": "5000 cm².", "C": "50000 cm².", "D": "500000 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (50000 cm²): Since 1 m squared equals 10000 cm squared (100 squared, not just 100), you multiply 5 by 10000 to get 50000 cm². Options A and B use the plain length conversion factor of 100 or 1000 instead of squaring it, and option D multiplies by 100000 by mistake."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylindrical fish tank has a radius of 10 cm and a height of 30 cm, as shown in the diagram. How many litres of water can it hold? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "94.2 L.", "B": "9.42 L.", "C": "942 L.", "D": "0.942 L."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (9.42 L): The tank's volume is pi times r squared times h, so 3.14 times 100 times 30 equals 9420 cm³, and since 1 litre equals 1000 cm³, dividing 9420 by 1000 gives 9.42 L. Option A divides by only 100 instead of 1000. Option C divides by only 10 instead of 1000, and option D divides by 10000 instead of 1000.", "image": "question_images/q_682.png"}, {"id": 683, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular storage box measures 25 cm by 20 cm by 15 cm, as shown in the diagram. How many litres of water could it hold if it were watertight?", "options": {"A": "750 L.", "B": "75 L.", "C": "7.5 L.", "D": "0.75 L."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (7.5 L): The box's volume is 25 times 20 times 15, equals 7500 cm³, and dividing by 1000 (since 1 L equals 1000 cm³) gives 7.5 L. Option A divides by only 10 instead of 1000. Option B divides by 100 instead of 1000, and option D divides by 10000 instead of 1000.", "image": "question_images/q_683.png"}, {"id": 684, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A closed cylindrical container has a radius of 7 cm and a height of 20 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is the total surface area of the container, including the top and bottom? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "879.2 cm².", "B": "307.72 cm².", "C": "1494.64 cm².", "D": "1186.92 cm²."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (1186.92 cm²): SA equals 2 times pi times r squared, plus 2 times pi times r times h, so 2 times 3.14 times 49, plus 2 times 3.14 times 7 times 20, equals 307.72 plus 879.2, equals 1186.92 cm². Option A is only the curved side area with the top and bottom left out. Option B is only the two circular ends with the curved side left out, and option C mistakenly counts the two circular ends twice, adding an extra 307.72 cm².", "image": "question_images/q_684.png"}, {"id": 686, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A square-based pyramid and a rectangular prism share the same base and the same height. If the prism's volume is 210 cubic centimetres, what is the pyramid's volume?", "options": {"A": "140 cm³.", "B": "105 cm³.", "C": "70 cm³.", "D": "630 cm³."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (70 cm³): A square-based pyramid always holds exactly one third the volume of a rectangular prism with the same base and height, so 210 divided by 3 equals 70 cm³. Option B divides by 2 instead of by 3. Option A is two thirds of the prism's volume instead of one third, and option D multiplies the prism's volume by 3 instead of dividing by it."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A sphere-shaped ball has a radius of 9 cm, as shown in the diagram. What is its volume? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "1017.36 cm³.", "B": "6104.16 cm³.", "C": "2289.06 cm³.", "D": "3052.08 cm³."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3052.08 cm³): The sphere volume formula is four thirds times pi times r cubed, so four thirds times 3.14 times 729, equals four thirds times 2289.06, equals 3052.08 cm³. Option A is actually the sphere's surface area, not its volume. Option C forgets the four thirds factor and just calculates pi times r cubed, and option B is double the correct volume.", "image": "question_images/q_687.png"}, {"id": 691, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Convert 7.2 m to centimetres.", "options": {"A": "7200 cm.", "B": "72 cm.", "C": "7.2 cm.", "D": "720 cm."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (720 cm): Since 1 m equals 100 cm, you multiply 7.2 by 100 to convert to centimetres, giving 720 cm. Option B keeps the same digits without multiplying by the correct power of ten. Option C leaves the measurement in metres instead of converting at all. Option A multiplies by 1000 instead of 100, which is the conversion factor for metres to kilometres, not metres to centimetres."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A container has a volume of 15000 cubic centimetres. How many litres is that?", "options": {"A": "1.5 L.", "B": "15 L.", "C": "15000 L.", "D": "150 L."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (15 L): Since 1000 cm³ equals 1 L, divide 15000 by 1000 to get 15 L. Option A divides by an extra factor of 10. Option D divides by only 100 instead of 1000. Option C forgets to convert at all and just repeats the cm³ value."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangle has a base of 14 cm and a height of 9 cm. What is its area?", "options": {"A": "23 cm².", "B": "63 cm².", "C": "126 cm².", "D": "46 cm²."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (63 cm²): Using A = one half × base × height, A = 0.5 × 14 × 9 = 63 cm². Option A adds the base and height instead of multiplying. Option D doubles that sum, matching a perimeter-style calculation instead. Option C multiplies base and height together but forgets to take one half of the result.", "image": "question_images/q_702.png"}, {"id": 703, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A parallelogram has a base of 11 cm and a perpendicular height of 7 cm. What is its area?", "options": {"A": "38.5 cm².", "B": "18 cm².", "C": "77 cm².", "D": "154 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (77 cm²): Using A = base × height, A = 11 × 7 = 77 cm². Option B adds the base and height instead of multiplying them. Option A mistakenly takes one half of the product, which is the triangle area formula, not the parallelogram formula. Option D doubles the correct area.", "image": "question_images/q_703.png"}, {"id": 706, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A composite shape looks like a rectangle with a triangle on top, like a house outline. The rectangle is 12 cm wide and 7 cm tall. The triangle on top has a base of 12 cm and a height of 5 cm. What is the total area of the shape?", "options": {"A": "84 cm².", "B": "30 cm².", "C": "114 cm².", "D": "144 cm²."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (114 cm²): The rectangle part has area 12 × 7 = 84 cm², and the triangle on top has area 0.5 × 12 × 5 = 30 cm². Adding the two pieces gives 84 + 30 = 114 cm². Option A only counts the rectangle and forgets the triangle on top. Option B only counts the triangle and forgets the rectangle. Option D incorrectly treats the whole shape as one big square.", "image": "question_images/q_706.png"}, {"id": 707, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A square piece of metal is 14 cm by 14 cm, with a circular hole of radius 4 cm cut from the centre. What is the remaining area? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "145.76 cm².", "B": "50.24 cm².", "C": "196 cm².", "D": "246.24 cm²."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (145.76 cm²): The square's area is 14 × 14 = 196 cm², and the circular hole's area is 3.14 × 4² = 50.24 cm². Since the hole is removed from the square, subtract: 196 minus 50.24 equals 145.76 cm². Option B only gives the area of the circular hole. Option C only gives the area of the square and forgets to subtract the hole. Option D mistakenly adds the two areas together instead of subtracting."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Which unit would correctly measure the area of a basketball court floor?", "options": {"A": "m.", "B": "m².", "C": "m³.", "D": "cm."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (m²): Area measures flat surface, so it is always given in square units like m². Option A is a unit of length, used for one-dimensional measurements like a side length. Option C is a unit of volume, used for three-dimensional space. Option D is also a unit of length, and it would be an oddly small unit to describe a whole basketball court."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular prism has a length of 6 cm, a width of 5 cm, and a height of 4 cm. What is its total surface area?", "options": {"A": "74 cm².", "B": "108 cm².", "C": "120 cm².", "D": "148 cm²."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (148 cm²): Using SA = 2 × (lw + lh + wh), SA = 2 × (30 + 24 + 20) = 2 × 74 = 148 cm². Option A stops after adding the three face areas but forgets to double the total for the matching pairs. Option B leaves out one of the three pairs of faces before doubling. Option C actually calculates the volume of the prism, not its surface area.", "image": "question_images/q_710.png"}, {"id": 713, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cone has a radius of 5 cm and a slant height of 13 cm. What is its total surface area? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "78.5 cm².", "B": "204.1 cm².", "C": "266.9 cm².", "D": "282.6 cm²."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (282.6 cm²): SA = π × r² + π × r × l = 3.14 × 25 + 3.14 × 5 × 13 = 78.5 + 204.1 = 282.6 cm². Option A only includes the circular base and forgets the curved side. Option B only includes the curved side and forgets the circular base. Option C mistakenly uses the cone's vertical height of 12 cm instead of the slant height for the curved side.", "image": "question_images/q_713.png"}, {"id": 716, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangular prism has a length of 7 cm, a width of 4 cm, and a height of 5 cm. What is its volume?", "options": {"A": "140 cm³.", "B": "16 cm³.", "C": "70 cm³.", "D": "166 cm³."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (140 cm³): Using V = length × width × height, V = 7 × 4 × 5 = 140 cm³. Option B adds the three dimensions instead of multiplying them. Option C is half of the correct volume. Option D actually matches a surface area style calculation, not the volume formula.", "image": "question_images/q_716.png"}, {"id": 721, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A sphere has a radius of 3 cm. What is its volume? Use pi equals 3.14.", "options": {"A": "28.26 cm³.", "B": "113.04 cm³.", "C": "84.78 cm³.", "D": "339.12 cm³."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (113.04 cm³): Using V = four thirds × π × r³, V = (4/3) × 3.14 × 27 = 113.04 cm³. Option A only calculates the area of a circle with this radius, not the volume of a sphere. Option C forgets the four thirds factor and only calculates π × r³ (3.14 × 27 = 84.78). Option D triples the correct volume.", "image": "question_images/q_721.png"}, {"id": 725, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "One cubic metre equals how many cubic centimetres?", "options": {"A": "1,000,000 cm³.", "B": "1,000 cm³.", "C": "100 cm³.", "D": "10,000 cm³."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1,000,000 cm³): Since 1 m equals 100 cm, 1 m³ equals 100 × 100 × 100, which is 1,000,000 cm³. Option C only uses the plain length conversion factor without cubing it. Option B cubes a factor of 10 instead of 100. Option D squares the factor of 100 instead of cubing it, which is the correct conversion for area, not volume."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Which rectangle has an area of 49 square metres and uses the least amount of fencing (the smallest perimeter)?", "options": {"A": "49 m by 1 m.", "B": "7 m by 7 m.", "C": "24.5 m by 2 m.", "D": "12.25 m by 4 m."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (7 m by 7 m): All four rectangles have an area of 49 m², but a square always needs the least perimeter for a fixed area, and 7 × 7 equals 49 with a perimeter of only 28 m. Option A is extremely stretched out and needs a perimeter of 100 m for the same area. Option C also needs more perimeter, 53 m, because it is less square-shaped. Option D needs 32.5 m of perimeter, still more than the true square."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A parallelogram has a base of 12 cm, a perpendicular height of 5 cm, and a slanted side of 7 cm. What is its area?", "options": {"A": "17 cm².", "B": "60 cm².", "C": "30 cm².", "D": "84 cm²."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (60 cm²): Using A = base × height, A = 12 × 5 = 60 cm². The slant length of 7 cm is extra information not needed for this formula. Option A adds the base and height instead of multiplying. Option C mistakenly takes one half of the product, which would be correct for a triangle but not a parallelogram. Option D mistakenly multiplies the base by the slant length instead of the perpendicular height.", "image": "question_images/q_731.png"}, {"id": 734, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A cylinder with a radius of 3 cm and a height of 9 cm has a volume of 254.34 cm³. What is the volume of a cone with the same radius and the same height?", "options": {"A": "84.78 cm³.", "B": "127.17 cm³.", "C": "508.68 cm³.", "D": "254.34 cm³."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (84.78 cm³): A cone always holds exactly one third the volume of a cylinder with the same radius and height, so 254.34 divided by 3 equals 84.78 cm³. Option D repeats the cylinder's own volume without adjusting for the cone shape. Option B mistakenly divides by 2 instead of 3. Option C doubles the cylinder's volume instead of taking one third of it.", "image": "question_images/q_734.png"}, {"id": 737, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Look at the graph below. What are the coordinates of point Q?", "options": {"A": "(-2, 4).", "B": "(4, -2).", "C": "(-4, 2).", "D": "(2, -4)."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ((4, -2)): point Q sits 4 units to the right of the origin and 2 units down, giving the ordered pair (x, y) equals (4, -2). Option A swaps the x-value and y-value. Option C flips the sign of both coordinates. Option D keeps the negative on the wrong value after swapping the two numbers.", "image": "question_images/q_737.png"}, {"id": 739, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Which ordered pair represents a point that is 5 units to the left of the origin and 3 units below the origin?", "options": {"A": "(-5, -3).", "B": "(-5, 3).", "C": "(5, -3).", "D": "(5, 3)."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ((-5, -3)): moving left from the origin makes the x-value negative, so x equals -5, and moving down makes the y-value negative, so y equals -3. Option C has the correct y-value but a wrong positive x-value. Option B has the correct x-value sign but a wrong positive y-value. Option D has both values positive, which does not match moving left and down at all."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A point has a positive x-coordinate and a negative y-coordinate. In which quadrant does this point lie?", "options": {"A": "Quadrant IV.", "B": "Quadrant II.", "C": "Quadrant III.", "D": "Quadrant I."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quadrant IV): Quadrant IV is defined as the region where x is positive and y is negative, which matches the point described. Quadrant I requires both values to be positive, so it does not fit. Quadrant II requires a negative x-value, which is the opposite of this point. Quadrant III requires both values to be negative, which also does not match."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangle has side lengths of 7 cm, 24 cm, and 25 cm, shown in the diagram below. Is this triangle a right triangle?", "options": {"A": "No, because 7 plus 24 does not equal 25.", "B": "Yes, because 7 squared plus 24 squared equals 25 squared.", "C": "Yes, because all three sides are different lengths.", "D": "No, because the sides are not all equal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because 7 squared plus 24 squared equals 25 squared): checking the Pythagorean theorem, 7 squared is 49 and 24 squared is 576, and 49 plus 576 equals 625, which is exactly 25 squared, so this confirms a right triangle. Option A compares the plain side lengths instead of their squares, which is not how the Pythagorean theorem works. Option C is true but does not prove the triangle is a right triangle, since many triangles have three different side lengths without being right triangles. Option D describes a rule for equilateral triangles, which is unrelated to checking for a right angle.", "image": "question_images/q_756.png"}, {"id": 759, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Two straight lines cross, forming four angles. One angle measures 65 degrees. What is the measure of the angle directly opposite it?", "options": {"A": "115 degrees.", "B": "25 degrees.", "C": "65 degrees.", "D": "180 degrees."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (65 degrees): angles directly opposite each other when two lines cross are called opposite or vertical angles, and they are always equal, so the opposite angle also measures 65 degrees. Option A is actually the measure of the two adjacent angles next to it, found using 180 minus 65, not the opposite angle. Option B comes from subtracting 65 from 90 instead of applying the vertical angle rule. Option D describes the sum of two angles on a straight line, not a single opposite angle."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Four angles meet at a single point. Three of them measure 90 degrees, 80 degrees, and 100 degrees. What is the measure of the fourth angle?", "options": {"A": "180 degrees.", "B": "90 degrees.", "C": "70 degrees.", "D": "110 degrees."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (90 degrees): angles around a single point always add up to 360 degrees, so the fourth angle is 360 minus 90 minus 80 minus 100, which equals 90 degrees. Option A wrongly uses 180 degrees as the total, which is the rule for a straight line, not a full point. Option C and option D both come from arithmetic slips while subtracting the three known angles from 360."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A quadrilateral has three known angles measuring 100 degrees, 85 degrees, and 95 degrees. What is the measure of the fourth angle?", "options": {"A": "80 degrees.", "B": "260 degrees.", "C": "90 degrees.", "D": "100 degrees."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (80 degrees): the interior angles of any quadrilateral always add up to 360 degrees, so the fourth angle is 360 minus 100 minus 85 minus 95, which equals 80 degrees. Option D repeats one of the given angles instead of solving for the missing one. Option B comes from subtracting only the 100 degree angle from 360 instead of all three known angles. Option C comes from an arithmetic slip during the subtraction."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A rectangle has two diagonals drawn from corner to corner. Which statement about these diagonals is true?", "options": {"A": "The diagonals are always different lengths.", "B": "The diagonals are always equal in length.", "C": "The diagonals cross at a right angle.", "D": "The diagonals never intersect."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The diagonals are always equal in length): a rectangle keeps every property of a parallelogram and adds the rule that its two diagonals are always equal in length. Option A directly contradicts this property of a rectangle. Option C describes a property of a rhombus, not a rectangle, since rhombus diagonals cross at a right angle. Option D is false because the diagonals of a rectangle always cross each other at the center of the shape."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point A is located at (2, 5), shown on the grid below. Point A is translated 4 units right and 3 units down. What are the coordinates of the translated point?", "options": {"A": "(6, 8).", "B": "(-2, 2).", "C": "(2, 6).", "D": "(6, 2)."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ((6, 2)): translating right means adding to the x-value, so 2 plus 4 equals 6, and translating down means subtracting from the y-value, so 5 minus 3 equals 2. Option A adds to both coordinates instead of subtracting for the downward movement. Option B subtracts from the x-value instead of adding, moving the point left instead of right. Option C never applies the 4-unit shift right, leaving the x-value unchanged, and mishandles the downward movement as well.", "image": "question_images/q_776.png"}, {"id": 777, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point B is located at (3, 7), shown on the grid below. Point B is reflected over the x-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?", "options": {"A": "(-3, 7).", "B": "(-3, -7).", "C": "(7, 3).", "D": "(3, -7)."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ((3, -7)): reflecting over the x-axis keeps the x-value the same and flips the sign of the y-value, so the new point is (3, -7). Option A wrongly flips the x-value instead of the y-value. Option B flips both values, which would be a 180 degree rotation instead of a reflection over the x-axis. Option C swaps the x-value and y-value entirely, which does not match a reflection at all.", "image": "question_images/q_777.png"}, {"id": 778, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point C is located at (-4, 6), shown on the grid below. Point C is reflected over the y-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?", "options": {"A": "(4, 6).", "B": "(4, -6).", "C": "(-4, 6).", "D": "(-4, -6)."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ((4, 6)): reflecting over the y-axis keeps the y-value the same and flips the sign of the x-value, so the new point is (4, 6). Option D wrongly flips the y-value instead of the x-value. Option B flips both values, which would be a 180 degree rotation instead of a reflection over the y-axis. Option C repeats the original point and applies no reflection at all.", "image": "question_images/q_778.png"}, {"id": 782, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Which axis on the Cartesian plane is the vertical number line?", "options": {"A": "The x-axis.", "B": "The diagonal axis.", "C": "The y-axis.", "D": "The origin line."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The y-axis): the y-axis is the vertical line that runs up and down through the grid. The x-axis is the horizontal line running left and right, so option A is incorrect. There is no such thing as a diagonal axis or an origin line on a standard Cartesian plane, making options B and D invalid terms."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point R is plotted on the grid below. Based on its position, which quadrant contains point R?", "options": {"A": "Quadrant I.", "B": "Quadrant III.", "C": "Quadrant II.", "D": "Quadrant IV."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Quadrant III): point R sits at (-6, -2), where both the x-value and y-value are negative. Quadrant III is defined as the region where both coordinates are negative, which is the bottom left section of the grid. Quadrants I, II, and IV each require at least one coordinate to be positive, so they do not match this point.", "image": "question_images/q_783.png"}, {"id": 784, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A point has a negative x-coordinate and a positive y-coordinate. Which quadrant contains this point?", "options": {"A": "Quadrant I.", "B": "Quadrant II.", "C": "Quadrant III.", "D": "Quadrant IV."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Quadrant II): Quadrant II is the top left region of the grid, where points sit to the left of the y-axis and above the x-axis, so x is negative and y is positive. Quadrant I has both coordinates positive. Quadrant III has both negative, and Quadrant IV has a positive x with a negative y."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "What is the distance between the points (0, 0) and (6, 8)?", "options": {"A": "48.", "B": "14.", "C": "10.", "D": "100."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (10): the horizontal difference is 6 and the vertical difference is 8. Squaring and adding gives 6 squared plus 8 squared, which is 36 plus 64, equal to 100, and the square root of 100 is 10. Option B simply adds the two differences instead of using the distance formula, option A multiplies the two differences (6 times 8 equals 48) instead of squaring and adding them, and option D stops at the squared sum of 100 and forgets the final square root.", "image": "question_images/q_785.png"}, {"id": 786, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Points M and N are plotted on the grid below at M(2, 1) and N(14, 6). What is the distance between M and N?", "options": {"A": "13.", "B": "12.", "C": "17.", "D": "169."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (13): the horizontal difference is 14 minus 2, which is 12, and the vertical difference is 6 minus 1, which is 5. Squaring and adding gives 12 squared plus 5 squared, which is 144 plus 25, equal to 169, and the square root of 169 is 13. Option B uses only the horizontal difference, option C simply adds 12 and 5, and option D forgets to take the final square root.", "image": "question_images/q_786.png"}, {"id": 792, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A ladder 15 feet long leans against a wall with its base sitting 9 feet away from the wall. The ladder, the wall, and the ground form a right triangle. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?", "options": {"A": "9 feet.", "B": "15 feet.", "C": "12 feet.", "D": "6 feet."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (12 feet): the ladder is the hypotenuse, so squaring it gives 15 squared equals 225, and squaring the base distance gives 9 squared equals 81. Subtracting gives 225 minus 81, which equals 144, and the square root of 144 is 12. Option A restates the base distance, option B restates the ladder length, and option D is simply the difference between 15 and 9 rather than the correct Pythagorean calculation."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "One angle measures 27 degrees. What is the measure of its complementary angle?", "options": {"A": "27 degrees.", "B": "153 degrees.", "C": "63 degrees.", "D": "73 degrees."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (63 degrees): complementary angles add up to 90 degrees, so the complement is 90 minus 27, which equals 63. Option B uses 180 instead of 90, option A repeats the given angle instead of finding its complement, and option D is a subtraction slip that does not equal 90 when added to 27."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Two straight lines cross, forming four angles. If one angle measures 38 degrees, what is the measure of the angle directly opposite it?", "options": {"A": "142 degrees.", "B": "52 degrees.", "C": "90 degrees.", "D": "38 degrees."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (38 degrees): angles directly opposite each other when two lines cross are called vertical angles, and vertical angles are always equal. Option A is actually the measure of the angles adjacent to the 38 degree angle (180 minus 38), option B mistakenly finds the complement instead of the vertical angle, and option C assumes a right angle that is not given."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. If one angle measures 63 degrees, what is the measure of its corresponding angle at the other intersection?", "options": {"A": "63 degrees.", "B": "27 degrees.", "C": "117 degrees.", "D": "153 degrees."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (63 degrees): corresponding angles sit in the same position at each intersection made by the transversal, and they are always equal to each other. Option B finds the complement instead of the corresponding angle, and options C and D are supplementary-style answers (180 minus 63 or a further miscalculation), which do not apply to corresponding angles."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangle has interior angles of 42 degrees and 88 degrees. What is the measure of the third angle?", "options": {"A": "50 degrees.", "B": "130 degrees.", "C": "46 degrees.", "D": "60 degrees."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (50 degrees): every triangle's interior angles add up to 180 degrees, so the third angle equals 180 minus 42 minus 88, which is 50. Option B is the sum of the two given angles rather than the remaining amount, option C is a subtraction slip, and option D does not make the three angles add up to 180."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangle has exactly two sides of equal length. What type of triangle is this?", "options": {"A": "Scalene.", "B": "Equilateral.", "C": "Isosceles.", "D": "Right."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Isosceles): an isosceles triangle is defined as having exactly two sides equal in length, along with two equal angles opposite those sides. A scalene triangle has all three sides different, so option A is wrong, an equilateral triangle has all three sides equal rather than just two, and a right triangle is classified by having a 90 degree angle, not by its side lengths."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "In an equilateral triangle, what is the measure of each interior angle?", "options": {"A": "60 degrees.", "B": "45 degrees.", "C": "90 degrees.", "D": "100 degrees."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (60 degrees): since all three angles in an equilateral triangle are equal and the total must be 180 degrees, each angle equals 180 divided by 3, which is 60. Option C describes a right angle, which does not fit an equilateral triangle, and options B and D do not divide evenly into three equal angles that sum to 180."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A quadrilateral has three known angles measuring 110 degrees, 95 degrees, and 80 degrees. What is the measure of the fourth angle?", "options": {"A": "65 degrees.", "B": "95 degrees.", "C": "85 degrees.", "D": "75 degrees."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (75 degrees): since a quadrilateral's angles must total 360 degrees, the fourth angle equals 360 minus 110 minus 95 minus 80, which is 75. Option A, C, and B each come from a small subtraction error and would not leave the four angles adding up correctly to 360."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point R is shown on the grid below at (7, -4). What are the coordinates of R after it is reflected over the x-axis?", "options": {"A": "(-7, 4).", "B": "(-7, -4).", "C": "(7, 4).", "D": "(7, -4)."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ((7, 4)): reflecting over the x-axis keeps the x-value the same and flips the sign of the y-value, so -4 becomes 4. Option B incorrectly flips the x-value instead of the y-value, option A flips both values, which describes a 180 degree rotation instead of a reflection, and option D shows no change at all.", "image": "question_images/q_813.png"}, {"id": 814, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point S is shown on the grid below at (-6, 9). What are the coordinates of S after it is reflected over the y-axis?", "options": {"A": "(-6, -9).", "B": "(6, -9).", "C": "(-6, 9).", "D": "(6, 9)."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ((6, 9)): reflecting over the y-axis keeps the y-value the same and flips the sign of the x-value, so -6 becomes 6. Option A flips the wrong coordinate, option C shows no change at all, and option B flips both coordinates, which describes a rotation rather than a reflection over the y-axis.", "image": "question_images/q_814.png"}, {"id": 818, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A TV screen has a width of 24 inches and a height of 18 inches, as shown below. What is the diagonal size of the TV?", "options": {"A": "30 inches.", "B": "900 inches.", "C": "42 inches.", "D": "21 inches."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (30 inches): the diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle formed by the width and height, so squaring gives 24 squared equals 576 and 18 squared equals 324. Adding gives 576 plus 324, which equals 900, and the square root of 900 is 30. Option B stops at the squared sum without taking the square root, option C simply adds 24 and 18, and option D is roughly half of the width plus height rather than the true diagonal.", "image": "question_images/q_818.png"}, {"id": 822, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A quadrilateral has two known angles of 90 degrees each, and the remaining two angles are equal to each other. What is the measure of each of the remaining equal angles?", "options": {"A": "85 degrees.", "B": "90 degrees.", "C": "95 degrees.", "D": "180 degrees."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (90 degrees): the total of all four angles must be 360 degrees, and the two known angles already total 180 degrees, leaving 180 degrees to be split evenly between the two equal angles, which gives 90 degrees each. Option A and C each come from a small arithmetic slip in splitting the remaining total, and option D is the full remaining total before splitting it in half."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Triangle XYZ is shown on the grid below with vertices X(0, 0), Y(3, 4), and Z(3, 0). What is the length of side XY?", "options": {"A": "4.", "B": "3.", "C": "7.", "D": "5."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (5): side XY connects X(0, 0) and Y(3, 4), so the horizontal difference is 3 and the vertical difference is 4. Squaring and adding gives 9 plus 16, which equals 25, and the square root of 25 is 5. Option A is only the vertical difference, option B is only the horizontal difference, and option C simply adds the two differences instead of applying the distance formula.", "image": "question_images/q_823.png"}, {"id": 826, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "What is the name of the vertical number line on the Cartesian plane?", "options": {"A": "The y-axis.", "B": "The x-axis.", "C": "The origin.", "D": "A quadrant line."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The y-axis): The y-axis is the vertical line on the Cartesian plane, running up and down. The x-axis is the horizontal line, not the vertical one. The origin is just the single point where the axes cross, not a whole line. A quadrant line is not a real term used in this chapter."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A point has a negative x-coordinate and a positive y-coordinate. Which quadrant is it located in?", "options": {"A": "Quadrant II.", "B": "Quadrant I.", "C": "Quadrant III.", "D": "Quadrant IV."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quadrant II): Quadrant II is defined as the region where x is negative and y is positive, which matches the point described. Quadrant I requires both coordinates to be positive. Quadrant III requires both coordinates to be negative. Quadrant IV requires a positive x-coordinate paired with a negative y-coordinate, the opposite of what is described."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "What is the complement of a 27 degree angle?", "options": {"A": "153 degrees.", "B": "63 degrees.", "C": "117 degrees.", "D": "27 degrees."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (63 degrees): Complementary angles add up to 90 degrees, so 90 minus 27 equals 63 degrees. 153 degrees comes from subtracting 27 from 180 instead of 90, finding the supplement rather than the complement. 117 degrees does not correspond to either the correct subtraction or a common formula. 27 degrees would mean the angle is its own complement, which only happens at exactly 45 degrees, not 27."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Two straight lines cross, and one of the angles formed measures 48 degrees. What is the measure of the angle directly opposite it?", "options": {"A": "132 degrees.", "B": "90 degrees.", "C": "42 degrees.", "D": "48 degrees."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (48 degrees): Angles directly opposite each other when two lines cross are called vertical angles, and vertical angles are always equal, so the opposite angle also measures 48 degrees. 132 degrees is actually the measure of the two angles adjacent to the 48 degree angle, found by 180 minus 48, not the opposite angle. 42 degrees does not relate to any correct angle relationship here. 90 degrees would only apply if the two lines crossed at a right angle, which is not stated."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "A triangle has angles measuring 42 degrees and 79 degrees. What is the measure of the third angle?", "options": {"A": "121 degrees.", "B": "69 degrees.", "C": "59 degrees.", "D": "101 degrees."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (59 degrees): Since the interior angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees, the third angle is 180 minus 42 minus 79, which equals 59 degrees. 121 degrees is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing third angle. 69 degrees and 101 degrees do not match the correct subtraction from 180 degrees."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "What happens to a point's coordinates when it is reflected over the x-axis?", "options": {"A": "The x-value stays the same and the sign of the y-value flips.", "B": "The y-value stays the same and the sign of the x-value flips.", "C": "The signs of both the x-coordinate and the y-coordinate flip completely to the opposite sign.", "D": "The x-value and y-value swap places entirely, with neither one flipping its sign at all."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The x-value stays the same and the sign of the y-value flips): Reflecting over the x-axis keeps the horizontal position the same while flipping the point to the opposite side vertically, which flips only the sign of the y-value. Keeping the y-value the same and flipping the x-value describes a reflection over the y-axis instead. Flipping both signs describes a 180 degree rotation about the origin, not a reflection. Swapping the x-value and y-value does not describe any of the standard reflections or rotations covered in this chapter."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "geometry_and_measurement", "question": "Point (7, -4) is shown on the coordinate grid. It is translated 5 units left and 3 units up. What are the coordinates of the new point?", "options": {"A": "(12, -7).", "B": "(2, -7).", "C": "(2, -1).", "D": "(12, -1)."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ((2, -1)): Moving 5 units left subtracts 5 from the x-value, giving 7 minus 5 equals 2, and moving 3 units up adds 3 to the y-value, giving -4 plus 3 equals -1. (12, -7) incorrectly adds 5 to the x-value and subtracts 3 from the y-value, the opposite directions. (2, -7) correctly moves the x-value left but incorrectly moves the y-value down instead of up. (12, -1) correctly moves the y-value up but incorrectly moves the x-value right instead of left.", "image": "question_images/q_867.png"}, {"id": 871, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "In the simple interest formula I = Prt, what does the variable r represent?", "options": {"A": "The total number of years the money is invested.", "B": "The original amount of money invested or borrowed.", "C": "The interest rate written as a decimal, such as 0.05.", "D": "The interest rate written as a percent, such as 5."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The interest rate written as a decimal, such as 0.05): In the formula I = Prt, r must be written as a decimal before it is used in a calculation, so 5 percent becomes 0.05. Option A describes t, not r. Option B describes P, the principal. Option D is wrong because using the percent form directly, like 5 instead of 0.05, gives an answer 100 times too large."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "In the formula I = Prt, what is the principal?", "options": {"A": "The interest rate applied each year.", "B": "The number of years money is invested or borrowed.", "C": "The final total amount after interest is added.", "D": "The original amount of money before any interest is added."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The original amount of money before any interest is added): The principal, P, is the starting amount of money, and with simple interest it never changes as interest builds up. Option A describes the rate, r. Option B describes the time, t. Option C describes the total amount, which is the principal plus the interest, not the principal itself."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Danielle invests $1000 at an annual simple interest rate of 5%. How much more interest does she earn after 4 years compared to after 2 years?", "options": {"A": "$50.", "B": "$100.", "C": "$200.", "D": "$250."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($100): Interest after 2 years is I = 1000 × 0.05 × 2 = 100, and interest after 4 years is I = 1000 × 0.05 × 4 = 200. The difference is 200 minus 100, which equals 100. Option A is only half the correct difference. Option C is the total interest after 4 years, not the difference between the two amounts. Option D does not match either correct calculation."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Find the final amount when $2000 is invested at an annual interest rate of 8%, compounded semi-annually, for 1 year.", "options": {"A": "$2,080.00.", "B": "$2,160.00.", "C": "$2,320.00.", "D": "$2,163.20."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($2,163.20): Semi-annual compounding means n = 2, so A = 2000(1 + 0.08/2)² = 2000(1.04)² = 2000 × 1.0816 = 2163.20. Option B is the simple interest total for one year (2000 + 2000 × 0.08 = 2160), which ignores the compounding. Option C overestimates the effect of compounding twice a year. Option A only applies a single 4% half-year period (2000 × 1.04) instead of two."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Trevor's monthly income is $2000. His fixed expenses total $800 and his variable expenses total $400. What are his total expenses for the month?", "options": {"A": "$1,200.", "B": "$400.", "C": "$1,600.", "D": "$800."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ($1,200): Total expenses equal fixed expenses plus variable expenses, so 800 + 400 = 1200. Option D only lists the fixed expenses. Option B only lists the variable expenses. Option C incorrectly adds an extra $400 that does not belong in the total."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Using the fact that 1.06²⁰ is approximately 3.207135, find the final amount when $500 is invested at 6% annual interest, compounded annually, for 20 years.", "options": {"A": "$3,207.14.", "B": "$1,603.57.", "C": "$500.00.", "D": "$1,000.00."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($1,603.57): A = P(1.06)²⁰ = 500 × 3.207135 = 1603.5675, which rounds to $1603.57. Option A is the answer for a $1000 investment, not $500. Option C repeats the principal without any growth. Option D doubles the principal without doing the actual calculation."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Why does starting to save money earlier in life generally lead to a much larger final amount, even with the same deposit and interest rate?", "options": {"A": "Banks pay a noticeably higher interest rate specifically to younger savers over time.", "B": "Earlier savings avoid all sales tax on any interest that is ever earned.", "C": "Simple interest automatically replaces compound interest once enough years have passed.", "D": "The money has more time for compound interest to build on itself."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The money has more time for compound interest to build on itself): More years invested means more compounding periods, so the balance grows faster and faster the longer it sits, as shown by Amara ending up with far more money than Ben. Option A is not how interest rates work. Option B is not a real rule about interest and tax. Option C is false since the type of interest does not change over time."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Sam has a $600 balance on a credit card with an annual interest rate of 24%, charged monthly. What is the interest charged for one month?", "options": {"A": "$144.00.", "B": "$12.00.", "C": "$2.00.", "D": "$24.00."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B ($12.00): The monthly rate is 24% divided by 12 months, which is 2%, or 0.02 as a decimal. Interest for one month is 600 × 0.02 = 12.00. Option A uses the full 24% annual rate for a single month instead of dividing it by 12. Option D repeats the annual percentage as a dollar amount without doing the calculation. Option C divides the rate incorrectly."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Sam owes $600 on a credit card, and this month's interest charge is $12.00. If Sam makes a minimum payment of $40, what is Sam's new balance?", "options": {"A": "$588.00.", "B": "$560.00.", "C": "$572.00.", "D": "$600.00."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($572.00): Of the $40 payment, $12.00 covers the interest, leaving 40 minus 12, which equals 28, to reduce the balance, so the new balance is 600 minus 28, which equals 572.00. Option B assumes the full $40 payment reduces the balance, ignoring the interest charge. Option A only subtracts the interest amount instead of the actual amount that reduces the balance. Option D assumes no progress was made at all."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "What does a person's credit score mainly reflect?", "options": {"A": "How much money the person currently earns per year.", "B": "How reliably the person pays back borrowed money.", "C": "How many credit cards the person owns in total.", "D": "How much sales tax the person has paid in their lifetime."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (How reliably the person pays back borrowed money): A credit score goes up when bills are paid on time and balances are kept low, and it goes down when payments are missed. Option A describes income, which is a different measure entirely. Option C is not how a credit score is calculated. Option D is unrelated to credit history."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "An item costs $120.00 before tax. Using the one-step method of multiplying by 1.13, what is the total price including HST?", "options": {"A": "$121.13.", "B": "$150.00.", "C": "$120.13.", "D": "$135.60."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($135.60): Multiplying the price by 1.13 finds 100% of the price plus the extra 13% tax in one step, so 120.00 × 1.13 = 135.60. Option A mistakenly adds only $1.13 to the price. Option C barely changes the price at all. Option B overestimates the total by using too high a rate."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "What is the current HST rate in Ontario?", "options": {"A": "5%.", "B": "13%.", "C": "8%.", "D": "15%."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (13%): Ontario's Harmonized Sales Tax is currently 13%, meaning $13 in tax is added for every $100 spent on a taxable item. Option A is closer to the federal GST rate used in some other provinces. Option C and D are not the correct HST rate for Ontario."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Which statement is most accurate about payday loans and buy-now-pay-later plans?", "options": {"A": "Payday loans often carry extremely high effective interest rates, and missed buy-now-pay-later payments can trigger steep fees.", "B": "Both are completely free and totally risk-free ways to borrow money, with no fees ever charged.", "C": "Buy-now-pay-later plans always report positively to a person's credit score with every single payment.", "D": "Payday loans are only ever available to people who already have excellent credit scores."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Payday loans often carry extremely high effective interest rates, and missed buy-now-pay-later payments can trigger steep fees): Payday loans can work out to a massive annual interest rate, and buy-now-pay-later plans can feel harmless but become risky if a payment is missed or tracked poorly. Option B ignores the real risks of both products. Option C is not guaranteed and is not how these plans generally work. Option D is backwards, since payday loans are typically marketed to people who may not qualify for other credit."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Before agreeing to any loan or payment plan, what does this chapter recommend doing first?", "options": {"A": "Only look at the size of the monthly payment and ignore every other part of the deal.", "B": "Assume every single offer with a low monthly payment is automatically the cheapest option overall.", "C": "Skip reading the full terms entirely whenever the loan amount or plan seems small enough.", "D": "Read the full terms, calculate the total cost, and ask if the purchase is a need or a want."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Read the full terms, calculate the total cost, and ask if the purchase is a need or a want): This chapter's general rule is to fully understand a loan before agreeing to it, including its true total cost. Option A ignores the total cost, which can hide extra interest. Option B is a common and risky assumption, since a lower payment can still mean a higher total cost. Option C could lead to missing important fees or conditions."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Vince earns $90 in simple interest on a $600 principal over 3 years. What annual interest rate did he earn?", "options": {"A": "50%.", "B": "0.05%.", "C": "5%.", "D": "15%."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (5%): Rearranging I = Prt gives r = I / (Pt), so r = 90 / (600 × 3) = 90 / 1800 = 0.05, which is 5%. Option A mistakes the decimal for a whole percent value 10 times too large. Option B places the decimal point in the wrong spot. Option D comes from an incorrect division."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A $400 investment grows to a total of $460 using simple interest at an annual rate of 5%. How many years was the money invested?", "options": {"A": "3 years.", "B": "5 years.", "C": "1 year.", "D": "12 years."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (3 years): The interest earned is 460 minus 400, which equals 60, and rearranging I = Prt gives t = I / (Pr) = 60 / (400 × 0.05) = 60 / 20 = 3. Option B does not match this calculation. Option C is far too short a time for that much interest to build up at 5%. Option D would produce a much larger amount of interest than $60."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Priya's monthly income is $2400, and she pays $960 in rent. What percent of her income goes toward rent?", "options": {"A": "96%.", "B": "60%.", "C": "4%.", "D": "40%."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (40%): Dividing rent by income gives 960 / 2400 = 0.4, which is 40%. Option A misplaces the decimal point. Option B is the percent that would be left over, not the percent spent on rent. Option C is far too small to match the actual numbers."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A credit card charges an annual interest rate of 18%, applied monthly. What is the monthly interest rate as a percent?", "options": {"A": "18%.", "B": "1.5%.", "C": "0.15%.", "D": "15%."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1.5%): Dividing the annual rate by 12 months gives 18 / 12 = 1.5%. Option A repeats the annual rate without dividing it. Option C misplaces the decimal point. Option D is not the result of dividing 18 by 12."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "What is interest, in the context of borrowing and saving money?", "options": {"A": "A fee that is charged only whenever a bill happens to be paid late.", "B": "The cost of borrowing money, or the reward for lending or saving it.", "C": "The original amount of money first deposited or borrowed in a bank account.", "D": "A type of sales tax that gets added onto the price of purchases."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The cost of borrowing money, or the reward for lending or saving it): This is the basic definition used throughout the chapter, whether a bank is paying interest to a saver or a borrower is paying interest to a lender. Option A describes a late fee, which is a different concept. Option C describes the principal, not interest. Option D confuses interest with sales tax, which is an unrelated system."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A savings account holds $700.00 at a simple interest rate of 6% per year. How much interest is earned after 4 years?", "options": {"A": "$16.80.", "B": "$868.00.", "C": "$168.00.", "D": "$42.00."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($168.00): using I = Prt, I = 700 × 0.06 × 4 = 168.00. Option A comes from mistakenly using a much smaller decimal for the rate. Option B is the total amount in the account (principal plus interest), not the interest alone. Option D only accounts for one year of interest and forgets to multiply by the 4 years."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A principal of $900.00 earns simple interest at 5% per year for 6 years. Find the total amount in the account after 6 years.", "options": {"A": "$1170.00.", "B": "$945.00.", "C": "$270.00.", "D": "$1080.00."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A ($1170.00): the interest is I = 900 × 0.05 × 6 = 270.00, and the total amount is P + I = 900 + 270 = 1170.00. Option C is only the interest earned, not the total amount requested. Option B and D come from using the wrong rate or forgetting to multiply by all 6 years. Always check whether a question asks for interest alone or the total amount."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "$1000.00 is invested at 10% annual interest, compounded semi-annually, for 1 year. Find the final amount.", "options": {"A": "$1100.00.", "B": "$1050.00.", "C": "$1105.00.", "D": "$1102.50."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($1102.50): since semi-annual compounding means n = 2, A = 1000(1 + 0.10/2)² = 1000(1.05)² = 1000 × 1.1025 = 1102.50. Option A comes from ignoring the semi-annual compounding and treating it as simple annual growth. Option B only applies the half-year rate one time instead of twice. Option C is a rounding error from an incorrect squaring step."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "$1200.00 is invested at 12% annual interest, compounded monthly, for 1 year. Find the final amount, rounded to the nearest cent.", "options": {"A": "$1344.00.", "B": "$1200.00.", "C": "$1352.19.", "D": "$1358.40."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($1352.19): with monthly compounding, r/n = 0.12/12 = 0.01 and nt = 12, so A = 1200(1.01)¹² = 1200 × 1.126825 = 1352.19. Option A is the result of using simple interest instead of compound interest. Option B shows no growth at all. Option D comes from an overestimated exponent calculation."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "$3000.00 is invested at 6% annual interest, compounded semi-annually, for 2 years. Find the final amount, rounded to the nearest cent.", "options": {"A": "$3360.00.", "B": "$3370.80.", "C": "$3180.00.", "D": "$3376.53."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($3376.53): with semi-annual compounding, r/n = 0.06/2 = 0.03 and the number of periods is 2 × 2 = 4, so A = 3000(1.03)⁴ = 3000 × 1.12550881 = 3376.53. Option A is the simple interest result, which ignores interest earned on interest. Option B compounds only once per year instead of twice. Option C only applies one year of growth at 6%."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A student calculates a savings account's monthly compound growth using A = P(1 + r)ⁿ, where r is the full annual rate and n is the number of months, without adjusting anything else. What correction is needed to fix this mistake?", "options": {"A": "multiply the annual rate by 12 first, before plugging it into the formula at all.", "B": "divide the annual rate by 12 and use the number of months as the exponent.", "C": "use n = 1 instead of the actual number of months that have passed.", "D": "add the annual rate directly to the principal first, without ever dividing it at all."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (divide the annual rate by 12 and use the number of months as the exponent): the rate must be divided by the compounding frequency to get r/n, and the exponent must equal nt, the total number of compounding periods. Option A makes the rate far too large instead of matching it to a monthly period. Option C removes the monthly compounding entirely, turning it back into a single annual calculation. Option D is not part of the compound interest formula at all."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "$500.00 is invested for 5 years at 8% annual interest. Compare simple interest to compound interest, compounded annually. How much more does the compound interest option earn than the simple interest option, rounded to the nearest cent?", "options": {"A": "$200.00.", "B": "$700.00.", "C": "$234.66.", "D": "$34.66."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D ($34.66): simple interest gives I = 500 × 0.08 × 5 = 200.00, for a total of 700.00. Compound interest gives A = 500(1.08)⁵ = 500 × 1.469328 = 734.66. The difference between the two totals is 734.66 - 700.00 = 34.66. Option A is only the simple interest amount, and Options B and C mix up totals and interest amounts instead of finding the actual difference between them."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Over a long period of time, compound interest will always ___ simple interest at the same rate and principal, assuming the rate is positive.", "options": {"A": "earn less than.", "B": "earn more than.", "C": "earn exactly the same as.", "D": "be impossible to compare with."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (earn more than): because compound interest earns interest on a growing balance while simple interest always earns interest only on the fixed original principal, compound interest pulls further ahead the longer the money sits. Option A reverses the true relationship between the two. Option C is only true at the very start, before any interest has been added. Option D is incorrect since both formulas can always be calculated and compared directly."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Why does compound interest grow faster than simple interest over time, when both start with the same rate and principal?", "options": {"A": "because the interest rate itself is mistakenly assumed to increase automatically every single year that passes by.", "B": "because compound interest is always mistakenly assumed to be given a much higher rate by the bank itself.", "C": "because compound interest is calculated on a balance that keeps growing, so each period earns interest on more money.", "D": "because simple interest is mistakenly assumed to stop being calculated entirely after the very first year passes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (because compound interest is calculated on a balance that keeps growing, so each period earns interest on more money): each compounding period adds interest to the balance, and the next period's interest is calculated on that larger amount. Option A is false, since the rate itself does not change, only the balance it is applied to. Option B incorrectly assumes banks always offer a higher rate for compound interest. Option D is false, since simple interest continues being calculated every year on the same fixed principal."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Why does starting to save earlier in life result in more money later, assuming the same amount saved and the same interest rate?", "options": {"A": "inflation automatically increases the value of money that was saved earlier in life.", "B": "banks pay younger savers a noticeably higher interest rate than older savers.", "C": "the money has more time for compound interest to build on itself.", "D": "simple interest works faster than compound interest for money saved earlier."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (the money has more time for compound interest to build on itself): the extra years allow the balance to compound repeatedly, producing a much larger final amount even without changing the rate or the amount saved. Option B is false, since banks do not adjust rates based on a saver's age. Option A incorrectly credits inflation for a result that is actually caused by interest. Option D is false, since simple interest grows at a constant rate no matter when it starts, so it does not explain the advantage of starting early."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "When is credit card interest usually NOT charged?", "options": {"A": "when the full balance is paid off every month.", "B": "when only the minimum payment is made.", "C": "when the card is maxed out.", "D": "credit cards always charge interest no matter what."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (when the full balance is paid off every month): paying the full balance each month usually avoids interest entirely, since nothing is carried over to the next billing period. Option B describes exactly the situation where interest does build up, since a balance is still being carried. Option C also involves carrying a balance, which triggers interest charges. Option D is false, since paying in full is a well known way to avoid credit card interest."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "What does a credit score reflect?", "options": {"A": "the exact dollar amount a person has in savings.", "B": "how reliably a person repays borrowed money.", "C": "a person's age and job title.", "D": "how many credit cards a person owns, regardless of payment history."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (how reliably a person repays borrowed money): a credit score goes up when bills are paid on time and balances stay low, and it goes down when payments are missed or balances stay high. Option A describes savings, which is a separate concept from borrowing and repayment history. Option C lists personal details that do not directly determine a credit score. Option D is false, since simply owning cards does not matter without considering how they are actually managed."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Why is a payday loan considered a risky way to borrow money?", "options": {"A": "it requires an excellent credit score to even qualify for the loan in the first place.", "B": "it always has a noticeably lower interest rate than a typical credit card would charge.", "C": "it charges extremely high fees that work out to a massive annual interest rate.", "D": "it automatically improves a person's credit score every single time it is used responsibly."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (it charges extremely high fees that work out to a massive annual interest rate): payday loans are short-term loans meant to be repaid by the next paycheque, but their fees often translate into an extremely high effective interest rate, sometimes several hundred percent per year. Option A is false, since payday loans are typically marketed as easy to access rather than requiring strong credit. Option B is the opposite of the truth, since payday loans usually cost far more than credit card interest. Option D is false, since there is no guarantee that using a payday loan improves a credit score."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Which statement correctly describes how simple interest is calculated over time?", "options": {"A": "It is calculated only on the original principal, which never changes.", "B": "It only applies to loans, never to savings accounts.", "C": "It is calculated on the current balance, which grows every year.", "D": "It compounds automatically once a year no matter what."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is calculated only on the original principal, which never changes): simple interest always uses the same starting principal for every calculation, which is what makes it simpler than compound interest. Option C describes compound interest instead, where the balance grows and future interest is calculated on that larger amount. Option B is false since simple interest can apply to both loans and savings, and option D confuses simple interest with compounding, which it does not do."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "What makes compound interest fundamentally different from simple interest?", "options": {"A": "Compound interest is assumed to only ever apply to credit card balances specifically.", "B": "The interest rate is assumed to change automatically every single year without reason.", "C": "Interest is earned on interest that has already been added to the balance.", "D": "The principal amount is assumed to decrease steadily as interest is added over time."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Interest is earned on interest that has already been added to the balance): compound interest recalculates interest on the growing balance instead of always using the original principal. Option A is false because compound interest is used by many savings and investment products, not only credit cards. Option B and D both describe things that do not actually happen in the compound interest formula, since the rate stays fixed and the principal itself does not shrink."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "A bank account pays 8% annual interest, compounded once a year. If Aisha deposits $1000 and leaves it for 2 years, what will the final amount be?", "options": {"A": "$1160.00.", "B": "$1180.00.", "C": "$1166.40.", "D": "$1080.00."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($1166.40): A = 1000(1 + 0.08/1)² = 1000(1.08)², and 1.08² = 1.1664, so A = 1000 × 1.1664 = 1166.40. Option A is the simple interest total for 2 years (1000 plus 160), which misses the $6.40 of interest earned on interest. Option B is a rounding or arithmetic slip that does not match the true value of 1.08 squared. Option D matches simple interest for one year only, not the compounded amount over two years."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "In budgeting, how is a need best described?", "options": {"A": "Something nice to have but not actually required to live or function day to day.", "B": "A type of savings account offered by a bank, unrelated to daily needs at all.", "C": "Any expense that simply changes in amount from one month to the next month.", "D": "Something required to live and function, like housing, food, or basic transportation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Something required to live and function, like housing, food, or basic transportation): needs are the essential expenses that must be covered before anything else in a budget. Option A actually describes a want, which is the opposite category from a need. Option B is unrelated to the need versus want distinction, and option C describes a variable expense, which is a different classification entirely."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Sofia has a monthly income of $2600 and total monthly expenses of $2200. What percent of her income is left over after paying her expenses, rounded to one decimal place?", "options": {"A": "15.4%.", "B": "10.0%.", "C": "20.0%.", "D": "84.6%."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (15.4%): her leftover amount is 2600 - 2200 = 400, and 400 divided by 2600 equals about 0.1538, which is 15.4% when rounded. Option D is actually the percentage her expenses take up, not her leftover amount, so it answers a different question. Options B and C are rounded guesses that do not match the actual division."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "Why are minimum payments on a credit card considered risky, even when a person makes the payment on time every month?", "options": {"A": "Minimum payments always pay off the entire balance within just a few short months.", "B": "Minimum payments are assumed to eliminate all future interest charges immediately upon payment.", "C": "A large part of the payment covers interest, so the balance shrinks very slowly.", "D": "Minimum payments automatically raise a person's credit limit every single month without exception."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A large part of the payment covers interest, so the balance shrinks very slowly): since interest is calculated on the balance before the payment is applied, only the leftover portion actually reduces what is owed. Option A and B both describe outcomes that do not happen with minimum payments, since they can take years to clear a balance and interest keeps accumulating. Option D is unrelated, since making a minimum payment does not change a credit limit at all."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "financial_literacy", "question": "An item is priced at $200. A 10% discount is applied first, and then HST is added to the discounted price. What is the final total the customer pays?", "options": {"A": "$200.00.", "B": "$226.00.", "C": "$203.40.", "D": "$180.00."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C ($203.40): the discount reduces the price to 200 × 0.90 = 180, and then the tax is applied to that discounted price, 180 × 0.13 = 23.40, giving a total of 180 + 23.40 = 203.40. Option D stops after the discount and forgets to add tax at all. Option B forgets to apply the 10% discount and just adds 13% tax to the original $200 price, and option A ignores both the discount and the tax."}];
